      "finite_checks": [],
      "finite_checks_disposition": "The argument is symbolic for all stated finite inputs; no separate numerical enumeration is needed.",
      "supplier_excerpts": [
        {
          "supplier": "def-qsm-dt-gksl-definition",
          "carrier": "items/def-qsm-dt-gksl-definition.md",
          "status": "Current carrier status: published; inspected for this dependency interface.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Fix a finite dimension $d\\geq1$, a positive constant $\\hbar$ in joule-seconds, a Hermitian matrix $H\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$ in joules, and a finite family $L_1,\\ldots,L_m\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$ with units ${\\rm s}^{-1/2}$, where $m\\geq0$. On dimensionless matrices $X\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$, define the **finite Gorini–Kossakowski–Sudarshan–Lindblad (GKSL) generator**\n\n$$\\mathcal L(X)=-\\frac{i}{\\hbar}[H,X]+\\sum_{a=1}^{m}\\left(L_aXL_a^*-\\frac12\\{L_a^*L_a,X\\}\\right),$$\n\nwhere $[A,B]=AB-BA$, $\\{A,B\\}=AB+BA$, and $A^*$ is the conjugate transpose. Each summand has units ${\\rm s}^{-1}$ times the units of $X$. An empty sum when $m=0$ is zero. The resulting $\\mathcal L$ is a linear map on the matrix space used in the finite-channel definition [[def-qsm-dt-channel-definition]].\n\nFor a compact time interval $I\\subset\\mathbb R$ whose coordinate is time in seconds, continuous functions $H:I\\to M_d(\\mathbb C)$ and $L_a:I\\to M_d(\\mathbb C)$ with $H(t)=H(t)^*$ define the corresponding **continuous finite GKSL family** $\\mathcal L_t$ by the same formula at each $t$. A propagator for this family means a family of linear maps $\\Lambda(t,s)$, $s\\leq t$ in $I$, satisfying $\\partial_t\\Lambda(t,s)=\\mathcal L_t\\circ\\Lambda(t,s)$ and $\\Lambda(s,s)=\\operatorname{id}$. This definition alone asserts neither existence nor uniqueness.\n\nThese are finite-dimensional mathematical definitions. They do not assert that a physical system follows this generator or that a microscopic bath has a Markovian limit.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact selected section above gives the interface; the consumer invokes it under its stated finite-dimensional hypotheses.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier status is published; this carrier has no proof section, as appropriate to its kind. No independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "Declared in the consumer reference interface; exact usage was inspected."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-qsm-matrix-state-calculus",
          "carrier": "items/lem-qsm-matrix-state-calculus.md",
          "status": "Current carrier status: published; inspected for this dependency interface.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Use the following conventions on a complex inner-product space $H$ of dimension $D\\geq1$, with inner product linear in its first argument. For an endomorphism $A$, define $\\operatorname{Tr}A:=\\sum_{j=1}^D\\langle Ae_j,e_j\\rangle$ in an orthonormal basis; step 3.1 proves basis independence. For any Hermitian $M$, its **positive spectral subspace** (called its positive support here) is $S_M:=\\bigoplus_{\\lambda>0}\\ker(M-\\lambda I)$; when $M\\geq0$ this equals $(\\ker M)^\\perp$. Its support logarithm is $\\log(M|_{S_M}):=\\sum_{\\lambda>0}(\\log\\lambda)P_\\lambda|_{S_M}$, where $P_\\lambda$ is the orthogonal projection onto that eigenspace. No value $\\log0$ is assigned; for $M=0$ this is the unique endomorphism of the zero space. For the set $\\mathcal D(H)$ of positive semidefinite Hermitian endomorphisms with trace one, a point $\\rho$ is **extreme** if every equality $\\rho=t\\sigma+(1-t)\\tau$ with $\\sigma,\\tau\\in\\mathcal D(H)$ and $0<t<1$ implies $\\sigma=\\tau=\\rho$.\n\nLet $H$ be a complex inner-product space of finite dimension $D\\geq1$, with inner product linear in its first argument. Every Hermitian endomorphism $M$ has an orthonormal eigenbasis with real eigenvalues. For a real function $f$ on its finite spectrum, define $f(M)$ by its spectral projections; this definition is independent of the choice of eigenbasis. In particular positive square roots, exponentials, and logarithms on positive supports are well defined.\n\nFor a density matrix $\\rho$ on $H$, meaning a Hermitian positive semidefinite operator with $\\operatorname{Tr}\\rho=1$, $\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho A)=\\sum_jp_j\\langle Au_j,u_j\\rangle$ for bounded $A$, where $\\rho=\\sum_jp_j|u_j\\rangle\\langle u_j|$. The weighted form $(X,Y)_\\rho=\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho X^*Y)$ satisfies Cauchy–Schwarz, $|(X,Y)_\\rho|^2\\leq(X,X)_\\rho(Y,Y)_\\rho$. Finally, $\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho^2)\\leq1$, with equality exactly for rank-one densities, and those are exactly the extreme points of the finite density-matrix set.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact selected section above gives the interface; the consumer invokes it under its stated finite-dimensional hypotheses.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier status is published; this carrier has no proof section, as appropriate to its kind. No independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "Declared in the consumer reference interface; exact usage was inspected."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "cor-finite-dimensional-normed-spaces-are-banach",
          "carrier": "items/cor-finite-dimensional-normed-spaces-are-banach.md",
          "status": "Current carrier status: published; inspected for this dependency interface.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $X$ be a normed space over $\\mathbb K\\in\\{\\mathbb R,\\mathbb C\\}$ and assume\n$X$ admits an ordered basis of finite length. Then $X$ is a Banach space in the\nsense of [[def-banach-space]].",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact selected section above gives the interface; the consumer invokes it under its stated finite-dimensional hypotheses.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier status is published; this carrier has no proof section, as appropriate to its kind. No independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "Declared in the consumer reference interface; exact usage was inspected."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-exponential-series-of-a-bounded-operator",
          "carrier": "items/lem-exponential-series-of-a-bounded-operator.md",
          "status": "Current carrier status: published; inspected for this dependency interface.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $X$ be a real or complex Banach space and let $A\\in\\mathcal B(X)$ ([[def-bounded-linear-operator]]). For $t\\in\\mathbb R$ define $E(t):=\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty\\frac{t^n}{n!}A^n$. Then the series converges absolutely in the operator norm $\\|\\cdot\\|$ of $\\mathcal B(X)$, uniformly for $t$ in compact subsets of $\\mathbb R$; $E(t)\\in\\mathcal B(X)$ with $\\|E(t)\\| \\le e^{|t|\\|A\\|}$; $E(0)=I$ and $E(t+s)=E(t)E(s)$ for all $s,t\\in\\mathbb R$; $t\\mapsto E(t)$ is of class $C^\\infty$ in the operator norm with $$E'(t)=AE(t)=E(t)A,$$ and $\\|E(t)-I-tA\\| \\le\\frac{t^2}{2}\\|A\\|^2e^{|t|\\|A\\|}$, so that $\\frac{E(t)-I}{t}\\to A$ in operator norm as $t\\to0$. In particular $E$ is a uniformly continuous (hence strongly continuous) group of bounded operators on $X$ whose generator is the bounded operator $A$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact selected section above gives the interface; the consumer invokes it under its stated finite-dimensional hypotheses.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier status is published; this carrier has no proof section, as appropriate to its kind. No independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "Declared in the consumer reference interface; exact usage was inspected."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-darboux-integral",
          "carrier": "items/def-darboux-integral.md",
          "status": "Current carrier status: published; inspected for this dependency interface.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $a < b$ be reals and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be bounded\n([[def-bounded-set]]). Write $\\mathcal{P}$ for the set of all partitions of\n$[a,b]$ ([[def-partition-and-refinement]]) and put\n\n$$\\mathcal{L} \\;:=\\; \\{\\, L(f,P) \\ : \\ P \\in \\mathcal{P} \\,\\}, \\qquad \\mathcal{U} \\;:=\\; \\{\\, U(f,P) \\ : \\ P \\in \\mathcal{P} \\,\\}$$\n\nfor the sets of lower and of upper Darboux sums ([[def-darboux-sums]]).\n\n### Both extrema exist\n\n**$\\mathcal{P}$ is nonempty**: the pair $(1, t)$ with $t_0 := a$ and $t_k := b$\nfor $k \\ge 1$ is a partition of $[a,b]$, since $a < b$. So $\\mathcal{L}$ and\n$\\mathcal{U}$ are nonempty.\n\n**$\\mathcal{L}$ is bounded above and $\\mathcal{U}$ is bounded below.** Fix any\n$Q \\in \\mathcal{P}$. By claim 2 of [[lem-refinement-inequalities]],\n$L(f,P) \\le U(f,Q)$ for every $P \\in \\mathcal{P}$, so $U(f,Q)$ is an upper bound\nof $\\mathcal{L}$; and $L(f,Q) \\le U(f,P)$ for every $P$, so $L(f,Q)$ is a lower\nbound of $\\mathcal{U}$.\n\nHence a nonempty set bounded above has a supremum\n([[def-complete-ordered-field]]) and a nonempty set bounded below has an infimum\n([[thm-infimum-property]], [[def-infimum]]), each unique ([[lem-sup-unique]]).\nThe **lower** and **upper Darboux integrals** of $f$ over $[a,b]$ are the real\nnumbers\n\n$$\\underline{\\int_a^b} f \\;:=\\; \\sup \\mathcal{L} \\;=\\; \\sup_{P} L(f,P), \\qquad \\overline{\\int_a^b} f \\;:=\\; \\inf \\mathcal{U} \\;=\\; \\inf_{P} U(f,P) .$$\n\n### The lower integral never exceeds the upper one\n\n$$\\underline{\\int_a^b} f \\;\\le\\; \\overline{\\int_a^b} f .$$\n\nIndeed, for each fixed $Q \\in \\mathcal{P}$ the number $U(f,Q)$ is an upper bound\nof $\\mathcal{L}$, so the least upper bound satisfies\n$\\underline{\\int_a^b} f \\le U(f,Q)$. As $Q$ was arbitrary, $\\underline{\\int_a^b}f$\nis a lower bound of $\\mathcal{U}$, and the greatest lower bound satisfies\n$\\underline{\\int_a^b} f \\le \\overline{\\int_a^b} f$ ([[def-infimum]]).\n\nMoreover, for **every** partition $P$,\n\n$$L(f,P) \\;\\le\\; \\underline{\\int_a^b} f \\;\\le\\; \\overline{\\int_a^b} f \\;\\le\\; U(f,P) ,$$\n\nthe outer inequalities because a member of a set is at most its supremum and at\nleast its infimum.\n\n### Integrability\n\n$f$ is **Darboux integrable on $[a,b]$**, and on this page simply\n**integrable**, when\n\n$$\\underline{\\int_a^b} f \\;=\\; \\overline{\\int_a^b} f ,$$\n\nand then the common value is written\n\n$$\\int_a^b f \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad \\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x ,$$\n\nthe **integral of $f$ over $[a,b]$**. It is a single well-determined real\nnumber, being the common value of two numbers each of which is unique\n([[lem-sup-unique]]). Without the displayed equality the symbol $\\int_a^b f$ is\nnot defined and is never written.\n\n**The inequality above is the whole difficulty.** By the previous paragraph\nintegrability is *never* a question of one integral exceeding the other, only of\nthe gap $\\overline{\\int_a^b} f - \\underline{\\int_a^b} f \\ge 0$ being $0$; and by\n[[thm-riemann-criterion]] that gap is $0$ exactly when a single partition can be\nfound making $U(f,P) - L(f,P)$ small. Whether that is possible is settled\ncompletely, in terms of the discontinuities of $f$, by\n[[thm-lebesgue-criterion]].\n\n**\"Riemann integrable\" means the same thing here.** The definition above is\nDarboux's. Riemann's own definition, in terms of tagged partitions of small\nmesh, is [[def-tagged-partition-and-riemann-sum]], and the two define the same\nclass of functions with the same integral by [[thm-darboux-equals-riemann]].\nUntil that theorem is proved the two phrases are kept apart; after it they are\nused interchangeably, as they are throughout the literature.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact selected section above gives the interface; the consumer invokes it under its stated finite-dimensional hypotheses.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier status is published; this carrier has no proof section, as appropriate to its kind. No independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "Declared in the consumer reference interface; exact usage was inspected."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-continuous-implies-integrable",
          "carrier": "items/thm-continuous-implies-integrable.md",
          "status": "Current carrier status: published; inspected for this dependency interface.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $a < b$ be reals and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be continuous on $[a,b]$\n(Continuity of $f : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ at a point of $A$ and on $A$: the $\\varepsilon$-$\\delta$ condition, its agreement with $\\lim_{x \\to c} f(x) = f(c)$ at a limit point, and continuity at an isolated point). Then $f$ is bounded (Lower bound, bounded below, bounded set) and Riemann\nintegrable on $[a,b]$ (The lower and upper Darboux integrals of a bounded $f$ on $[a,b]$ as $\\sup_P L(f,P)$ and $\\inf_P U(f,P)$, Darboux integrability as their equality, and the notation $\\int_a^b f$).\n\n**The proof gives more than integrability: it gives a partition that works.** For\nevery real $\\varepsilon > 0$ the uniform partition into $N$ parts already\nsatisfies $U(f,P) - L(f,P) < \\varepsilon$, as soon as $N$ is large enough that\n$(b-a)/\\iota(N)$ is below the $\\delta$ that uniform continuity supplies for\n$\\varepsilon/\\bigl(2(b-a)\\bigr)$. Uniform continuity is exactly what makes one\n$\\delta$ serve all $N$ subintervals at once, and it is the only place where the\ncompactness of $[a,b]$ is used.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "The exact selected section above gives the interface; the consumer invokes it under its stated finite-dimensional hypotheses.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier status is published; this carrier has no proof section, as appropriate to its kind. No independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "Declared in the consumer reference interface; exact usage was inspected."
        }
      ],
      "risk_review": {
        "status": "complete",
        "notes": "Checked each exact declared interface, its hypotheses and conventions, and its consuming use. This Step 1 record is not independent proof approval."
