    "pthm-nrqmx-finite-reduction-channel": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "post-nrqmx-finite-open-model",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Work with finite-dimensional system and environment spaces $H_S=\\mathbb C^d$ and $H_E=\\mathbb C^m$, where $d,m\\geq1$, using the ordered tensor product $H_S\\otimes H_E$ with the system factor first. A system or environment state is a positive semidefinite Hermitian matrix of trace one; these state matrices are dimensionless. At the initial time, for every allowed system state $\\rho$, adopt the joint preparation $\\rho\\otimes\\eta$, where the fixed environment state $\\eta$ does not depend on $\\rho$. For each $t\\geq0$, choose a declared unitary $U_t$ on $H_S\\otimes H_E$, meaning $U_t^*U_t=U_tU_t^*=I$, with $U_0=I$. The reduced system dynamics is the map $\\Phi_t(\\rho)=\\operatorname{Tr}_E\\!\\left(U_t(\\rho\\otimes\\eta)U_t^*\\right)$, where for the fixed product basis the finite partial trace has entries $(\\operatorname{Tr}_E R)_{\\alpha\\beta}=\\sum_{j=1}^mR_{(\\alpha,j),(\\beta,j)}$. This assignment excludes initially correlated joint states. It imposes no one-parameter group, continuity, or semigroup law on $U_t$.\n\nA time-homogeneous finite GKSL model may instead be selected as a separate approximation: choose a fixed finite-dimensional Hamiltonian matrix $H=H^*$ in joules, a constant $\\hbar>0$ in joule-seconds, and finitely many jump matrices $L_a$ in $\\mathrm{s}^{-1/2}$, and use $T_t=e^{t\\mathcal L}$ for $t\\geq0$ with $\\mathcal L(X)=-\\frac{i}{\\hbar}[H,X]+\\sum_a\\left(L_aXL_a^*-\\frac12\\{L_a^*L_a,X\\}\\right)$. Each application must state its rates and physical regime. Selecting this approximation is not a consequence of the finite unitary reduction, and this postulate claims no weak-coupling or reservoir limit.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qc-partial-trace",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a complex vector space $H$, write $\\operatorname{End}(H)$ for the set of\nlinear maps $H\\to H$ ([[def-linear-map]]).\n\nLet $H_A,H_B$ be finite-dimensional complex inner-product spaces with fixed\nordered orthonormal bases $(e_i)_{i<a}$ and $(f_j)_{j<b}$, respectively. The\nordered product basis of $H_A\\otimes H_B$ has the $A$ index first. For\n$R\\in\\operatorname{End}(H_A\\otimes H_B)$ define\n$\\operatorname{Tr}_B(R)\\in\\operatorname{End}(H_A)$ by\n\n$$\\bigl(\\operatorname{Tr}_B(R)\\bigr)_{i i'}:=\\sum_{j<b}R_{(i,j),(i',j)}.$$\n\nRows are output coordinates and columns are input coordinates, as in\n[[def-coordinate-column-and-matrix-of-a-linear-map]]. If $b=0$, the sum is\nempty and equals $0$. The notation $\\operatorname{Tr}_B$ means that the second\nfactor is contracted; the first factor and its order are retained.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-qc-partial-trace-characterization-and-basis-independence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For finite-dimensional complex inner-product spaces $H_A,H_B$, the partial\ntrace $\\operatorname{Tr}_B:\\operatorname{End}(H_A\\otimes H_B)\\to\n\\operatorname{End}(H_A)$ is the unique linear map satisfying\n\n$$\\operatorname{tr}\\bigl((\\operatorname{Tr}_B R)\\circ T\\bigr)=\\operatorname{tr}\\bigl(R\\circ(T\\otimes I_{H_B})\\bigr)\\quad\\text{for every }R\\in\\operatorname{End}(H_A\\otimes H_B)\\text{ and }T\\in\\operatorname{End}(H_A).$$\n\nHere $I_{H_B}$ is the identity map $y\\mapsto y$ on $H_B$; $\\operatorname{End}$\nis the notation defined in [[def-qc-partial-trace]].\n\nIts coordinate formula is independent of the chosen ordered orthonormal basis\nof $H_B$. The assertion includes zero-dimensional factors.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-qsm-matrix-state-calculus",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Use the following conventions on a complex inner-product space $H$ of dimension $D\\geq1$, with inner product linear in its first argument. For an endomorphism $A$, define $\\operatorname{Tr}A:=\\sum_{j=1}^D\\langle Ae_j,e_j\\rangle$ in an orthonormal basis; step 3.1 proves basis independence. For any Hermitian $M$, its **positive spectral subspace** (called its positive support here) is $S_M:=\\bigoplus_{\\lambda>0}\\ker(M-\\lambda I)$; when $M\\geq0$ this equals $(\\ker M)^\\perp$. Its support logarithm is $\\log(M|_{S_M}):=\\sum_{\\lambda>0}(\\log\\lambda)P_\\lambda|_{S_M}$, where $P_\\lambda$ is the orthogonal projection onto that eigenspace. No value $\\log0$ is assigned; for $M=0$ this is the unique endomorphism of the zero space. For the set $\\mathcal D(H)$ of positive semidefinite Hermitian endomorphisms with trace one, a point $\\rho$ is **extreme** if every equality $\\rho=t\\sigma+(1-t)\\tau$ with $\\sigma,\\tau\\in\\mathcal D(H)$ and $0<t<1$ implies $\\sigma=\\tau=\\rho$.\n\nLet $H$ be a complex inner-product space of finite dimension $D\\geq1$, with inner product linear in its first argument. Every Hermitian endomorphism $M$ has an orthonormal eigenbasis with real eigenvalues. For a real function $f$ on its finite spectrum, define $f(M)$ by its spectral projections; this definition is independent of the choice of eigenbasis. In particular positive square roots, exponentials, and logarithms on positive supports are well defined.\n\nFor a density matrix $\\rho$ on $H$, meaning a Hermitian positive semidefinite operator with $\\operatorname{Tr}\\rho=1$, $\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho A)=\\sum_jp_j\\langle Au_j,u_j\\rangle$ for bounded $A$, where $\\rho=\\sum_jp_j|u_j\\rangle\\langle u_j|$. The weighted form $(X,Y)_\\rho=\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho X^*Y)$ satisfies Cauchy–Schwarz, $|(X,Y)_\\rho|^2\\leq(X,X)_\\rho(Y,Y)_\\rho$. Finally, $\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho^2)\\leq1$, with equality exactly for rank-one densities, and those are exactly the extreme points of the finite density-matrix set.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-qsm-dt-channel-definition",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $d,e\\geq1$ be integers, let $H_{\\mathrm{in}}=\\mathbb C^d$ and $H_{\\mathrm{out}}=\\mathbb C^e$ with their standard inner products, and write $M_d(\\mathbb C)$ and $M_e(\\mathbb C)$ for the complex linear maps on these spaces. A matrix $X$ is **positive semidefinite**, written $X\\geq0$, when it is Hermitian and $\\langle Xv,v\\rangle\\geq0$ for every vector $v$. The finite spectral convention in [[lem-qsm-matrix-state-calculus]] gives positive matrices their orthonormal eigenbases and positive square roots.\n\nA **positive map** is a complex-linear map $\\Phi:M_d(\\mathbb C)\\to M_e(\\mathbb C)$ such that $\\Phi(X)\\geq0$ whenever $X\\geq0$. For each integer $r\\geq1$, identify $M_r(\\mathbb C)\\otimes M_d(\\mathbb C)$ with matrices on $\\mathbb C^r\\otimes\\mathbb C^d$ in the ordered product basis. The map $\\Phi$ is **completely positive** when $\\operatorname{id}_r\\otimes\\Phi$ is positive for every such $r$, where $\\operatorname{id}_r$ is the identity map on $M_r(\\mathbb C)$.\n\nThe map $\\Phi$ is **trace-preserving** when $\\operatorname{Tr}(\\Phi(X))=\\operatorname{Tr}(X)$ for every $X\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$. A **finite quantum channel** is a complex-linear map that is both completely positive and trace-preserving, abbreviated CPTP. Unitality, $\\Phi(I_d)=I_e$, is a separate condition and is not part of this definition.\n\nThis is a mathematical definition. A physical preparation, measurement, or open-system model must separately identify when one of these maps represents a physical transformation.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-qsm-dt-choi-kraus",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $d,e\\geq1$ and let $\\Phi:M_d(\\mathbb C)\\to M_e(\\mathbb C)$ be complex-linear. Choose the standard input basis $(|i\\rangle)_{i=1}^d$, let $E_{ij}=|i\\rangle\\langle j|$, and use the ordered tensor convention input-reference $\\otimes$ output. Define the (unnormalized) Choi matrix\n\n$$J(\\Phi):=\\sum_{i,j=1}^{d}E_{ij}\\otimes\\Phi(E_{ij})\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)\\otimes M_e(\\mathbb C).$$ Then $\\Phi$ is completely positive if and only if $J(\\Phi)\\geq0$, and this holds if and only if there are $q\\leq de$ matrices $K_1,\\ldots,K_q:\\mathbb C^d\\to\\mathbb C^e$ such that $$\\Phi(X)=\\sum_{a=1}^{q}K_aXK_a^*\\qquad(X\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)).$$\n\nThe empty sum is allowed when $\\Phi=0$. For any such Kraus representation, $\\Phi$ is trace-preserving if and only if $\\sum_{a=1}^{q}K_a^*K_a=I_d$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-nrqmx-finite-channel-families",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $d\\geq1$ and let $\\Phi_t:M_d(\\mathbb C)\\to M_d(\\mathbb C)$ be a family of finite quantum channels indexed by $t\\geq0$, where “finite quantum channel” has the meaning in [[def-qsm-dt-channel-definition]]. The family obeys the **semigroup law** when $\\Phi_0=\\operatorname{id}_{M_d(\\mathbb C)}$ and $\\Phi_{t+s}=\\Phi_t\\circ\\Phi_s$ for every $s,t\\geq0$.\n\nThe family is **CP-divisible** when for every $0\\leq s\\leq t$ there exists a finite quantum channel $V_{t,s}:M_d(\\mathbb C)\\to M_d(\\mathbb C)$ such that $\\Phi_t=V_{t,s}\\circ\\Phi_s$. CP-divisibility is this specified factorization property; the definition does not assert that it is a universal meaning of “Markovian”.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-complex-exponential",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\iota_{\\mathbb R}\\colon\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb R$ be the canonical-natural map\nof [[def-canonical-natural]], and let $j\\colon\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb C$ be the real\nembedding $j(x)=x+i0$ from [[def-complex-numbers-and-arithmetic]]. For every\n$n\\in\\mathbb N$, the factorial $n!$ is nonzero by\n[[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]], so\n$\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!)>0$ by [[lem-of-naturals-positive]] and consequently\n$j(\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!))\\ne0$ in the complex field\n[[thm-complex-numbers-form-a-field]].\n\nFor $z\\in\\mathbb C$, define\n$$ \\exp z:=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{z^n}{j(\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!))}. $$\nwhenever this complex series converges. Inside a complex expression we\nabbreviate the embedded denominator $j(\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!))$ by $n!$, so the\nsame definition may be written $\\exp z=\\sum z^n/n!$ without identifying a\nnatural number with a complex number. Powers and series are those of\n[[def-complex-integer-powers]] and\n[[def-complex-series-power-series-and-absolute-convergence]]. The convergence\nfor every $z\\in\\mathbb C$ is discharged by\n[[lem-complex-exponential-series-converges-everywhere]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-complex-exponential-addition-and-real-extension",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For all $z,w\\in\\mathbb C$, $\\exp(z+w)=\\exp z\\exp w$. For real $x$, the complex value $\\exp(x+0i)$ equals the published real exponential $e^x$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[def-complex-exponential]], [[lem-complex-exponential-series-converges-everywhere]], [[lem-cauchy-product-of-absolutely-convergent-complex-series]], [[def-real-exponential-function-and-e]], [[lem-binomial-theorem-over-complex-numbers]], [[thm-binomial-closed-formula]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For real $x,y$, $\\exp(x+iy)=e^x(\\cos y+i\\sin y)$ and $|\\exp(x+iy)|=e^x$. In particular $e^{i\\pi}+1=0$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[thm-complex-exponential-addition-and-real-extension]], [[thm-eulers-formula]], [[lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws]], [[thm-pythagorean-and-parity-identities-for-all-six-trigonometric-functions]], [[cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity]], [[thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas]], [[def-pi-via-first-positive-cosine-zero]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "thm-sine-and-cosine-parametrize-the-unit-circle",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The map $t\\mapsto(\\cos t,\\sin t)$ is a bijection from $[0,2\\pi)$ onto $S^1=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:x^2+y^2=1\\}$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity]], [[thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges]], [[thm-sine-cosine-zero-sets-and-fundamental-period]], [[def-pi-via-first-positive-cosine-zero]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "By [F3], choose an orthonormal environment eigenbasis $(|k\\rangle)_{k=1}^m$ with $\\eta=\\sum_{k=1}^mp_k|k\\rangle\\langle k|$, $p_k\\geq0$, and $\\sum_kp_k=1$. Choose any system orthonormal basis $(|\\alpha\\rangle)_{\\alpha=1}^d$ and use their ordered product basis. By [F2], this basis change leaves the finite partial trace in [F1] unchanged. For environment indices $j,k$ define the system matrix $K_{jk}$ by $(K_{jk})_{\\alpha\\beta}:=\\sqrt{p_k}(U_t)_{(\\alpha,j),(\\beta,k)}$. Expanding $X\\otimes\\eta$, conjugating by $U_t$, and applying the coordinate formula [F1] gives, entry by entry, $\\Phi_t(X)=\\sum_{j,k=1}^mK_{jk}XK_{jk}^*$ for every $X\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "For a two-qubit instance take $Z=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)$, $g>0$ in $\\mathrm{s}^{-1}$, $H_{\\mathrm{tot}}=\\hbar g\\,Z\\otimes Z$ with $\\hbar$ in joule-seconds, and $\\eta=|+\\rangle\\langle+|$ where $|+\\rangle=(|0\\rangle+|1\\rangle)/\\sqrt2$ in the chosen environment $Z$ eigenbasis. By [F3], the finite spectral exponential gives $U_t=e^{-igtZ\\otimes Z}=\\sum_{b=\\pm1}V_b(t)\\otimes|b\\rangle\\langle b|$, with $V_b(t)=\\operatorname{diag}(e^{-ibgt},e^{ibgt})$. The partial trace in [F1] therefore gives $\\Phi_t(X)=\\frac12(V_{+1}(t)XV_{+1}(t)^*+V_{-1}(t)XV_{-1}(t)^*)$, so for $X=\\begin{pmatrix}a&z\\\\w&c\\end{pmatrix}$ the diagonal entries are unchanged and the off-diagonal entries are multiplied by $\\kappa(t)=\\frac12(e^{-2igt}+e^{2igt})=\\cos(2gt)$, using [F7], [F8], and [F9].",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "F9",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "step": "1.3",
          "claim": "By [F10], choose $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$ with $\\cos\\theta=0$ and $\\sin\\theta=1$; hence $\\theta>0$. Set $s=\\theta/(2g)>0$. Euler's identity [F9] gives $e^{i\\theta}=i$, and [F7]–[F8] give $e^{-i\\theta}=(e^{i\\theta})^{-1}=-i$. Applying [F8] again yields $\\kappa(s)=0$ and $\\kappa(2s)=\\frac12(e^{-2i\\theta}+e^{2i\\theta})=-1$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "F9",
            "F10",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "For system basis indices $\\beta,\\delta$, unitarity of $U_t$ gives $(\\sum_{j,k}K_{jk}^*K_{jk})_{\\beta\\delta}=\\sum_{k=1}^mp_k\\sum_{j=1}^m\\sum_{\\alpha=1}^d\\overline{(U_t)_{(\\alpha,j),(\\beta,k)}}(U_t)_{(\\alpha,j),(\\delta,k)}=\\sum_kp_k\\delta_{\\beta\\delta}=\\delta_{\\beta\\delta}$. Thus $\\sum_{j,k}K_{jk}^*K_{jk}=I_d$, so [F5] makes the Kraus map in step 1.1 completely positive and trace-preserving; by [F4], $\\Phi_t$ is a finite channel.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Let $X_{\\!P}=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\rho_\\pm=(I\\pm X_{\\!P})/2$. Since $X_{\\!P}=X_{\\!P}^*$ and $X_{\\!P}^2=I$, each $\\rho_\\pm$ is a projection; since $\\operatorname{Tr}X_{\\!P}=0$, each has trace one. Steps 1.2 and 1.3 give $\\Phi_s(\\rho_+)=\\Phi_s(\\rho_-)=I/2$, while $\\Phi_{2s}(\\rho_+)=\\rho_-$ and $\\Phi_{2s}(\\rho_-)=\\rho_+$. Consequently $\\Phi_{2s}(\\rho_+)\\ne\\Phi_s(\\Phi_s(\\rho_+))=I/2$, so the semigroup law in [F6] fails. If a channel $V_{2s,s}$ satisfied $\\Phi_{2s}=V_{2s,s}\\circ\\Phi_s$, it would send the equal matrices $\\Phi_s(\\rho_+)$ and $\\Phi_s(\\rho_-)$ to equal outputs, contradicting the two distinct outputs at $2s$. By step 2.1, the family consists of channels, so this contradiction proves it is not CP-divisible by [F6].",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F6",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 1.3",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
