      "finite_checks_disposition": "Exact finite matrix and boundary substitutions are recorded; these check the displayed finite examples and supplement the symbolic proof.",
      "supplier_excerpts": [
        {
          "supplier": "post-nrqmx-finite-open-model",
          "carrier": "items/post-nrqmx-finite-open-model.md",
          "status": "Current carrier status: draft; inspected for this dependency interface.",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "exact_statement": "Work with finite-dimensional system and environment spaces $H_S=\\mathbb C^d$ and $H_E=\\mathbb C^m$, where $d,m\\geq1$, using the ordered tensor product $H_S\\otimes H_E$ with the system factor first. A system or environment state is a positive semidefinite Hermitian matrix of trace one; these state matrices are dimensionless. At the initial time, for every allowed system state $\\rho$, adopt the joint preparation $\\rho\\otimes\\eta$, where the fixed environment state $\\eta$ does not depend on $\\rho$. For each $t\\geq0$, choose a declared unitary $U_t$ on $H_S\\otimes H_E$, meaning $U_t^*U_t=U_tU_t^*=I$, with $U_0=I$. The reduced system dynamics is the map $\\Phi_t(\\rho)=\\operatorname{Tr}_E\\!\\left(U_t(\\rho\\otimes\\eta)U_t^*\\right)$, where for the fixed product basis the finite partial trace has entries $(\\operatorname{Tr}_E R)_{\\alpha\\beta}=\\sum_{j=1}^mR_{(\\alpha,j),(\\beta,j)}$. This assignment excludes initially correlated joint states. It imposes no one-parameter group, continuity, or semigroup law on $U_t$.\n\nA time-homogeneous finite GKSL model may instead be selected as a separate approximation: choose a fixed finite-dimensional Hamiltonian matrix $H=H^*$ in joules, a constant $\\hbar>0$ in joule-seconds, and finitely many jump matrices $L_a$ in $\\mathrm{s}^{-1/2}$, and use $T_t=e^{t\\mathcal L}$ for $t\\geq0$ with $\\mathcal L(X)=-\\frac{i}{\\hbar}[H,X]+\\sum_a\\left(L_aXL_a^*-\\frac12\\{L_a^*L_a,X\\}\\right)$. Each application must state its rates and physical regime. Selecting this approximation is not a consequence of the finite unitary reduction, and this postulate claims no weak-coupling or reservoir limit.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Applicable consumer restatements: A1: [A1] [[post-nrqmx-finite-open-model]]: $\\eta$ is fixed independently of every allowed input $\\rho$, the joint initial state is $\\rho\\otimes\\eta$, and the reduced map is $\\Phi_t(X)=\\operatorname{Tr}_E(U_t(X\\otimes\\eta)U_t^*)$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier status is draft; the exact current interface was inspected; proof is not applicable to this carrier.",
          "consumer_use": "A1 is used in proof steps 1.1, 1.2"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-nrqmx-finite-channel-families",
          "carrier": "items/def-nrqmx-finite-channel-families.md",
          "status": "Current carrier status: draft; inspected for this dependency interface.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $d\\geq1$ and let $\\Phi_t:M_d(\\mathbb C)\\to M_d(\\mathbb C)$ be a family of finite quantum channels indexed by $t\\geq0$, where “finite quantum channel” has the meaning in [[def-qsm-dt-channel-definition]]. The family obeys the **semigroup law** when $\\Phi_0=\\operatorname{id}_{M_d(\\mathbb C)}$ and $\\Phi_{t+s}=\\Phi_t\\circ\\Phi_s$ for every $s,t\\geq0$.\n\nThe family is **CP-divisible** when for every $0\\leq s\\leq t$ there exists a finite quantum channel $V_{t,s}:M_d(\\mathbb C)\\to M_d(\\mathbb C)$ such that $\\Phi_t=V_{t,s}\\circ\\Phi_s$. CP-divisibility is this specified factorization property; the definition does not assert that it is a universal meaning of “Markovian”.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Applicable consumer restatements: F6: [F6] [[def-nrqmx-finite-channel-families]]: CP-divisibility requires a finite channel $V_{t,s}$ for every $s\\leq t$ with $\\Phi_t=V_{t,s}\\circ\\Phi_s$; the semigroup law requires $\\Phi_{t+s}=\\Phi_t\\circ\\Phi_s$ and $\\Phi_0=\\operatorname{id}$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier status is draft; the exact current interface was inspected; proof is not applicable to this carrier.",
          "consumer_use": "F6 is used in proof steps 3.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-qc-partial-trace",
          "carrier": "items/def-qc-partial-trace.md",
          "status": "Current carrier status: published; inspected for this dependency interface.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "For a complex vector space $H$, write $\\operatorname{End}(H)$ for the set of\nlinear maps $H\\to H$ ([[def-linear-map]]).\n\nLet $H_A,H_B$ be finite-dimensional complex inner-product spaces with fixed\nordered orthonormal bases $(e_i)_{i<a}$ and $(f_j)_{j<b}$, respectively. The\nordered product basis of $H_A\\otimes H_B$ has the $A$ index first. For\n$R\\in\\operatorname{End}(H_A\\otimes H_B)$ define\n$\\operatorname{Tr}_B(R)\\in\\operatorname{End}(H_A)$ by\n\n$$\\bigl(\\operatorname{Tr}_B(R)\\bigr)_{i i'}:=\\sum_{j<b}R_{(i,j),(i',j)}.$$\n\nRows are output coordinates and columns are input coordinates, as in\n[[def-coordinate-column-and-matrix-of-a-linear-map]]. If $b=0$, the sum is\nempty and equals $0$. The notation $\\operatorname{Tr}_B$ means that the second\nfactor is contracted; the first factor and its order are retained.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Applicable consumer restatements: F1: [F1] [[def-qc-partial-trace]]: in the ordered product basis, $(\\operatorname{Tr}_E R)_{\\alpha\\beta}=\\sum_jR_{(\\alpha,j),(\\beta,j)}$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier status is published; this carrier has no proof section, as appropriate to its kind. No independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F1 is used in proof steps 1.1, 1.2"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-qc-partial-trace-characterization-and-basis-independence",
          "carrier": "items/thm-qc-partial-trace-characterization-and-basis-independence.md",
          "status": "Current carrier status: published; inspected for this dependency interface.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "For finite-dimensional complex inner-product spaces $H_A,H_B$, the partial\ntrace $\\operatorname{Tr}_B:\\operatorname{End}(H_A\\otimes H_B)\\to\n\\operatorname{End}(H_A)$ is the unique linear map satisfying\n\n$$\\operatorname{tr}\\bigl((\\operatorname{Tr}_B R)\\circ T\\bigr)=\\operatorname{tr}\\bigl(R\\circ(T\\otimes I_{H_B})\\bigr)\\quad\\text{for every }R\\in\\operatorname{End}(H_A\\otimes H_B)\\text{ and }T\\in\\operatorname{End}(H_A).$$\n\nHere $I_{H_B}$ is the identity map $y\\mapsto y$ on $H_B$; $\\operatorname{End}$\nis the notation defined in [[def-qc-partial-trace]].\n\nIts coordinate formula is independent of the chosen ordered orthonormal basis\nof $H_B$. The assertion includes zero-dimensional factors.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Applicable consumer restatements: F2: [F2] [[thm-qc-partial-trace-characterization-and-basis-independence]]: the finite partial trace is characterized by the trace pairing and its coordinate map is independent of the chosen environment orthonormal basis.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier status is published; this carrier has no proof section, as appropriate to its kind. No independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F2 is used in proof steps 1.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-qsm-dt-channel-definition",
          "carrier": "items/def-qsm-dt-channel-definition.md",
          "status": "Current carrier status: published; inspected for this dependency interface.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $d,e\\geq1$ be integers, let $H_{\\mathrm{in}}=\\mathbb C^d$ and $H_{\\mathrm{out}}=\\mathbb C^e$ with their standard inner products, and write $M_d(\\mathbb C)$ and $M_e(\\mathbb C)$ for the complex linear maps on these spaces. A matrix $X$ is **positive semidefinite**, written $X\\geq0$, when it is Hermitian and $\\langle Xv,v\\rangle\\geq0$ for every vector $v$. The finite spectral convention in [[lem-qsm-matrix-state-calculus]] gives positive matrices their orthonormal eigenbases and positive square roots.\n\nA **positive map** is a complex-linear map $\\Phi:M_d(\\mathbb C)\\to M_e(\\mathbb C)$ such that $\\Phi(X)\\geq0$ whenever $X\\geq0$. For each integer $r\\geq1$, identify $M_r(\\mathbb C)\\otimes M_d(\\mathbb C)$ with matrices on $\\mathbb C^r\\otimes\\mathbb C^d$ in the ordered product basis. The map $\\Phi$ is **completely positive** when $\\operatorname{id}_r\\otimes\\Phi$ is positive for every such $r$, where $\\operatorname{id}_r$ is the identity map on $M_r(\\mathbb C)$.\n\nThe map $\\Phi$ is **trace-preserving** when $\\operatorname{Tr}(\\Phi(X))=\\operatorname{Tr}(X)$ for every $X\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$. A **finite quantum channel** is a complex-linear map that is both completely positive and trace-preserving, abbreviated CPTP. Unitality, $\\Phi(I_d)=I_e$, is a separate condition and is not part of this definition.\n\nThis is a mathematical definition. A physical preparation, measurement, or open-system model must separately identify when one of these maps represents a physical transformation.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Applicable consumer restatements: F4: [F4] [[def-qsm-dt-channel-definition]]: a finite channel is a complex-linear map that is completely positive on every finite ancilla extension and trace-preserving on every matrix input.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier status is published; this carrier has no proof section, as appropriate to its kind. No independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F4 is used in proof steps 2.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-qsm-dt-choi-kraus",
          "carrier": "items/thm-qsm-dt-choi-kraus.md",
          "status": "Current carrier status: published; inspected for this dependency interface.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $d,e\\geq1$ and let $\\Phi:M_d(\\mathbb C)\\to M_e(\\mathbb C)$ be complex-linear. Choose the standard input basis $(|i\\rangle)_{i=1}^d$, let $E_{ij}=|i\\rangle\\langle j|$, and use the ordered tensor convention input-reference $\\otimes$ output. Define the (unnormalized) Choi matrix\n\n$$J(\\Phi):=\\sum_{i,j=1}^{d}E_{ij}\\otimes\\Phi(E_{ij})\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)\\otimes M_e(\\mathbb C).$$ Then $\\Phi$ is completely positive if and only if $J(\\Phi)\\geq0$, and this holds if and only if there are $q\\leq de$ matrices $K_1,\\ldots,K_q:\\mathbb C^d\\to\\mathbb C^e$ such that $$\\Phi(X)=\\sum_{a=1}^{q}K_aXK_a^*\\qquad(X\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)).$$\n\nThe empty sum is allowed when $\\Phi=0$. For any such Kraus representation, $\\Phi$ is trace-preserving if and only if $\\sum_{a=1}^{q}K_a^*K_a=I_d$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Applicable consumer restatements: F5: [F5] [[thm-qsm-dt-choi-kraus]]: a finite map of Kraus form $\\Psi(X)=\\sum_aK_aXK_a^*$ is completely positive, and it is trace-preserving exactly when $\\sum_aK_a^*K_a=I$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier status is published; this carrier has no proof section, as appropriate to its kind. No independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F5 is used in proof steps 2.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-qsm-matrix-state-calculus",
          "carrier": "items/lem-qsm-matrix-state-calculus.md",
          "status": "Current carrier status: published; inspected for this dependency interface.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Use the following conventions on a complex inner-product space $H$ of dimension $D\\geq1$, with inner product linear in its first argument. For an endomorphism $A$, define $\\operatorname{Tr}A:=\\sum_{j=1}^D\\langle Ae_j,e_j\\rangle$ in an orthonormal basis; step 3.1 proves basis independence. For any Hermitian $M$, its **positive spectral subspace** (called its positive support here) is $S_M:=\\bigoplus_{\\lambda>0}\\ker(M-\\lambda I)$; when $M\\geq0$ this equals $(\\ker M)^\\perp$. Its support logarithm is $\\log(M|_{S_M}):=\\sum_{\\lambda>0}(\\log\\lambda)P_\\lambda|_{S_M}$, where $P_\\lambda$ is the orthogonal projection onto that eigenspace. No value $\\log0$ is assigned; for $M=0$ this is the unique endomorphism of the zero space. For the set $\\mathcal D(H)$ of positive semidefinite Hermitian endomorphisms with trace one, a point $\\rho$ is **extreme** if every equality $\\rho=t\\sigma+(1-t)\\tau$ with $\\sigma,\\tau\\in\\mathcal D(H)$ and $0<t<1$ implies $\\sigma=\\tau=\\rho$.\n\nLet $H$ be a complex inner-product space of finite dimension $D\\geq1$, with inner product linear in its first argument. Every Hermitian endomorphism $M$ has an orthonormal eigenbasis with real eigenvalues. For a real function $f$ on its finite spectrum, define $f(M)$ by its spectral projections; this definition is independent of the choice of eigenbasis. In particular positive square roots, exponentials, and logarithms on positive supports are well defined.\n\nFor a density matrix $\\rho$ on $H$, meaning a Hermitian positive semidefinite operator with $\\operatorname{Tr}\\rho=1$, $\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho A)=\\sum_jp_j\\langle Au_j,u_j\\rangle$ for bounded $A$, where $\\rho=\\sum_jp_j|u_j\\rangle\\langle u_j|$. The weighted form $(X,Y)_\\rho=\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho X^*Y)$ satisfies Cauchy–Schwarz, $|(X,Y)_\\rho|^2\\leq(X,X)_\\rho(Y,Y)_\\rho$. Finally, $\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho^2)\\leq1$, with equality exactly for rank-one densities, and those are exactly the extreme points of the finite density-matrix set.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Applicable consumer restatements: F3: [F3] [[lem-qsm-matrix-state-calculus]]: every finite positive matrix has an orthonormal eigenbasis with nonnegative eigenvalues, and its trace is the sum of its diagonal entries in any orthonormal basis.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier status is published; this carrier has no proof section, as appropriate to its kind. No independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F3 is used in proof steps 1.1, 1.2, 3.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-complex-exponential",
          "carrier": "items/def-complex-exponential.md",
          "status": "Current carrier status: published; inspected for this dependency interface.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $\\iota_{\\mathbb R}\\colon\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb R$ be the canonical-natural map\nof [[def-canonical-natural]], and let $j\\colon\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb C$ be the real\nembedding $j(x)=x+i0$ from [[def-complex-numbers-and-arithmetic]]. For every\n$n\\in\\mathbb N$, the factorial $n!$ is nonzero by\n[[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]], so\n$\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!)>0$ by [[lem-of-naturals-positive]] and consequently\n$j(\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!))\\ne0$ in the complex field\n[[thm-complex-numbers-form-a-field]].\n\nFor $z\\in\\mathbb C$, define\n$$ \\exp z:=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{z^n}{j(\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!))}. $$\nwhenever this complex series converges. Inside a complex expression we\nabbreviate the embedded denominator $j(\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!))$ by $n!$, so the\nsame definition may be written $\\exp z=\\sum z^n/n!$ without identifying a\nnatural number with a complex number. Powers and series are those of\n[[def-complex-integer-powers]] and\n[[def-complex-series-power-series-and-absolute-convergence]]. The convergence\nfor every $z\\in\\mathbb C$ is discharged by\n[[lem-complex-exponential-series-converges-everywhere]].",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Applicable consumer restatements: F7: [F7] [[def-complex-exponential]]: $\\exp z=\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty z^n/n!$ and $\\exp 0=1$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier status is published; this carrier has no proof section, as appropriate to its kind. No independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F7 is used in proof steps 1.2, 1.3"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-complex-exponential-addition-and-real-extension",
          "carrier": "items/thm-complex-exponential-addition-and-real-extension.md",
          "status": "Current carrier status: published; inspected for this dependency interface.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "For all $z,w\\in\\mathbb C$, $\\exp(z+w)=\\exp z\\exp w$. For real $x$, the complex value $\\exp(x+0i)$ equals the published real exponential $e^x$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[def-complex-exponential]], [[lem-complex-exponential-series-converges-everywhere]], [[lem-cauchy-product-of-absolutely-convergent-complex-series]], [[def-real-exponential-function-and-e]], [[lem-binomial-theorem-over-complex-numbers]], [[thm-binomial-closed-formula]].",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Applicable consumer restatements: F8: [F8] [[thm-complex-exponential-addition-and-real-extension]]: $\\exp(z+w)=\\exp z\\,\\exp w$ for complex $z,w$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier status is published; this carrier has no proof section, as appropriate to its kind. No independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F8 is used in proof steps 1.2, 1.3"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity",
          "carrier": "items/cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity.md",
          "status": "Current carrier status: published; inspected for this dependency interface.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "For real $x,y$, $\\exp(x+iy)=e^x(\\cos y+i\\sin y)$ and $|\\exp(x+iy)|=e^x$. In particular $e^{i\\pi}+1=0$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[thm-complex-exponential-addition-and-real-extension]], [[thm-eulers-formula]], [[lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws]], [[thm-pythagorean-and-parity-identities-for-all-six-trigonometric-functions]], [[cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity]], [[thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas]], [[def-pi-via-first-positive-cosine-zero]].",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Applicable consumer restatements: F9: [F9] [[cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity]]: for real $\\theta$, $\\exp(i\\theta)=\\cos\\theta+i\\sin\\theta$ and $|\\exp(i\\theta)|=1$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier status is published; this carrier has no proof section, as appropriate to its kind. No independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F9 is used in proof steps 1.2, 1.3"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-sine-and-cosine-parametrize-the-unit-circle",
          "carrier": "items/thm-sine-and-cosine-parametrize-the-unit-circle.md",
          "status": "Current carrier status: published; inspected for this dependency interface.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "The map $t\\mapsto(\\cos t,\\sin t)$ is a bijection from $[0,2\\pi)$ onto $S^1=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:x^2+y^2=1\\}$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity]], [[thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges]], [[thm-sine-cosine-zero-sets-and-fundamental-period]], [[def-pi-via-first-positive-cosine-zero]].",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Applicable consumer restatements: F10: [F10] [[thm-sine-and-cosine-parametrize-the-unit-circle]]: there exists $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$ with $(\\cos\\theta,\\sin\\theta)=(0,1)$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier status is published; this carrier has no proof section, as appropriate to its kind. No independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F10 is used in proof steps 1.3"
        }
      ],
      "risk_review": {
        "status": "complete",
        "notes": "Checked each exact declared interface, its hypotheses and conventions, and its consuming use. This Step 1 record is not independent proof approval."
      },
      "repair_confidence": 1
    },
    "pthm-nrqmx-amplitude-damping-model": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "post-nrqmx-finite-open-model",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Work with finite-dimensional system and environment spaces $H_S=\\mathbb C^d$ and $H_E=\\mathbb C^m$, where $d,m\\geq1$, using the ordered tensor product $H_S\\otimes H_E$ with the system factor first. A system or environment state is a positive semidefinite Hermitian matrix of trace one; these state matrices are dimensionless. At the initial time, for every allowed system state $\\rho$, adopt the joint preparation $\\rho\\otimes\\eta$, where the fixed environment state $\\eta$ does not depend on $\\rho$. For each $t\\geq0$, choose a declared unitary $U_t$ on $H_S\\otimes H_E$, meaning $U_t^*U_t=U_tU_t^*=I$, with $U_0=I$. The reduced system dynamics is the map $\\Phi_t(\\rho)=\\operatorname{Tr}_E\\!\\left(U_t(\\rho\\otimes\\eta)U_t^*\\right)$, where for the fixed product basis the finite partial trace has entries $(\\operatorname{Tr}_E R)_{\\alpha\\beta}=\\sum_{j=1}^mR_{(\\alpha,j),(\\beta,j)}$. This assignment excludes initially correlated joint states. It imposes no one-parameter group, continuity, or semigroup law on $U_t$.\n\nA time-homogeneous finite GKSL model may instead be selected as a separate approximation: choose a fixed finite-dimensional Hamiltonian matrix $H=H^*$ in joules, a constant $\\hbar>0$ in joule-seconds, and finitely many jump matrices $L_a$ in $\\mathrm{s}^{-1/2}$, and use $T_t=e^{t\\mathcal L}$ for $t\\geq0$ with $\\mathcal L(X)=-\\frac{i}{\\hbar}[H,X]+\\sum_a\\left(L_aXL_a^*-\\frac12\\{L_a^*L_a,X\\}\\right)$. Each application must state its rates and physical regime. Selecting this approximation is not a consequence of the finite unitary reduction, and this postulate claims no weak-coupling or reservoir limit.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qsm-dt-gksl-definition",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a finite dimension $d\\geq1$, a positive constant $\\hbar$ in joule-seconds, a Hermitian matrix $H\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$ in joules, and a finite family $L_1,\\ldots,L_m\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$ with units ${\\rm s}^{-1/2}$, where $m\\geq0$. On dimensionless matrices $X\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$, define the **finite Gorini–Kossakowski–Sudarshan–Lindblad (GKSL) generator**\n\n$$\\mathcal L(X)=-\\frac{i}{\\hbar}[H,X]+\\sum_{a=1}^{m}\\left(L_aXL_a^*-\\frac12\\{L_a^*L_a,X\\}\\right),$$\n\nwhere $[A,B]=AB-BA$, $\\{A,B\\}=AB+BA$, and $A^*$ is the conjugate transpose. Each summand has units ${\\rm s}^{-1}$ times the units of $X$. An empty sum when $m=0$ is zero. The resulting $\\mathcal L$ is a linear map on the matrix space used in the finite-channel definition [[def-qsm-dt-channel-definition]].\n\nFor a compact time interval $I\\subset\\mathbb R$ whose coordinate is time in seconds, continuous functions $H:I\\to M_d(\\mathbb C)$ and $L_a:I\\to M_d(\\mathbb C)$ with $H(t)=H(t)^*$ define the corresponding **continuous finite GKSL family** $\\mathcal L_t$ by the same formula at each $t$. A propagator for this family means a family of linear maps $\\Lambda(t,s)$, $s\\leq t$ in $I$, satisfying $\\partial_t\\Lambda(t,s)=\\mathcal L_t\\circ\\Lambda(t,s)$ and $\\Lambda(s,s)=\\operatorname{id}$. This definition alone asserts neither existence nor uniqueness.\n\nThese are finite-dimensional mathematical definitions. They do not assert that a physical system follows this generator or that a microscopic bath has a Markovian limit.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "cor-finite-dimensional-normed-spaces-are-banach",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a normed space over $\\mathbb K\\in\\{\\mathbb R,\\mathbb C\\}$ and assume\n$X$ admits an ordered basis of finite length. Then $X$ is a Banach space in the\nsense of [[def-banach-space]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-exponential-series-of-a-bounded-operator",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a real or complex Banach space and let $A\\in\\mathcal B(X)$ ([[def-bounded-linear-operator]]). For $t\\in\\mathbb R$ define $E(t):=\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty\\frac{t^n}{n!}A^n$. Then the series converges absolutely in the operator norm $\\|\\cdot\\|$ of $\\mathcal B(X)$, uniformly for $t$ in compact subsets of $\\mathbb R$; $E(t)\\in\\mathcal B(X)$ with $\\|E(t)\\| \\le e^{|t|\\|A\\|}$; $E(0)=I$ and $E(t+s)=E(t)E(s)$ for all $s,t\\in\\mathbb R$; $t\\mapsto E(t)$ is of class $C^\\infty$ in the operator norm with $$E'(t)=AE(t)=E(t)A,$$ and $\\|E(t)-I-tA\\| \\le\\frac{t^2}{2}\\|A\\|^2e^{|t|\\|A\\|}$, so that $\\frac{E(t)-I}{t}\\to A$ in operator norm as $t\\to0$. In particular $E$ is a uniformly continuous (hence strongly continuous) group of bounded operators on $X$ whose generator is the bounded operator $A$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-complex-exponential",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\iota_{\\mathbb R}\\colon\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb R$ be the canonical-natural map\nof [[def-canonical-natural]], and let $j\\colon\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb C$ be the real\nembedding $j(x)=x+i0$ from [[def-complex-numbers-and-arithmetic]]. For every\n$n\\in\\mathbb N$, the factorial $n!$ is nonzero by\n[[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]], so\n$\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!)>0$ by [[lem-of-naturals-positive]] and consequently\n$j(\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!))\\ne0$ in the complex field\n[[thm-complex-numbers-form-a-field]].\n\nFor $z\\in\\mathbb C$, define\n$$ \\exp z:=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{z^n}{j(\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!))}. $$\nwhenever this complex series converges. Inside a complex expression we\nabbreviate the embedded denominator $j(\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!))$ by $n!$, so the\nsame definition may be written $\\exp z=\\sum z^n/n!$ without identifying a\nnatural number with a complex number. Powers and series are those of\n[[def-complex-integer-powers]] and\n[[def-complex-series-power-series-and-absolute-convergence]]. The convergence\nfor every $z\\in\\mathbb C$ is discharged by\n[[lem-complex-exponential-series-converges-everywhere]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-qsm-dt-finite-matrix-trace-ideal",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal H$ be a complex Hilbert space of finite dimension $d\\ge1$. For a matrix $X$ define $|X|=(X^*X)^{1/2}$ by finite Hermitian functional calculus and define its trace norm by $\\|X\\|_1=\\operatorname{Tr}|X|$. This is a complete norm on the finite-dimensional matrix space. Write $\\|Y\\|:=\\sup\\{\\|Yv\\|:\\|v\\|\\le1\\}$ for the operator norm induced by the Hilbert-space norm. For all matrices $X,Y$,\n$$\n\\|YX\\|_1\\le\\|Y\\|\\|X\\|_1,\\qquad \\|XY\\|_1\\le\\|Y\\|\\|X\\|_1,\\qquad |\\operatorname{Tr}X|\\le\\|X\\|_1.\n$$\nFor unitary $U$, $\\|UXU^*\\|_1=\\|X\\|_1$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-qsm-dt-gksl-sufficient",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal L$ be a finite GKSL generator on $M_d(\\mathbb C)$, $d\\geq1$, with the units and conventions of [[def-qsm-dt-gksl-definition]]. For a bounded operator $A$ on a complex Banach space, write $e^{tA}:=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}t^nA^n/n!$ for the operator-norm convergent series of [[lem-exponential-series-of-a-bounded-operator]]. Then $T_t=e^{t\\mathcal L}$ is a finite channel for every $t\\geq0$. The family satisfies $T_0=\\operatorname{id}$, $T_{t+s}=T_t\\circ T_s$, and is norm-continuous. Time is measured in seconds; no negative-time channel or physical bath limit is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-qsm-dt-channel-definition",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $d,e\\geq1$ be integers, let $H_{\\mathrm{in}}=\\mathbb C^d$ and $H_{\\mathrm{out}}=\\mathbb C^e$ with their standard inner products, and write $M_d(\\mathbb C)$ and $M_e(\\mathbb C)$ for the complex linear maps on these spaces. A matrix $X$ is **positive semidefinite**, written $X\\geq0$, when it is Hermitian and $\\langle Xv,v\\rangle\\geq0$ for every vector $v$. The finite spectral convention in [[lem-qsm-matrix-state-calculus]] gives positive matrices their orthonormal eigenbases and positive square roots.\n\nA **positive map** is a complex-linear map $\\Phi:M_d(\\mathbb C)\\to M_e(\\mathbb C)$ such that $\\Phi(X)\\geq0$ whenever $X\\geq0$. For each integer $r\\geq1$, identify $M_r(\\mathbb C)\\otimes M_d(\\mathbb C)$ with matrices on $\\mathbb C^r\\otimes\\mathbb C^d$ in the ordered product basis. The map $\\Phi$ is **completely positive** when $\\operatorname{id}_r\\otimes\\Phi$ is positive for every such $r$, where $\\operatorname{id}_r$ is the identity map on $M_r(\\mathbb C)$.\n\nThe map $\\Phi$ is **trace-preserving** when $\\operatorname{Tr}(\\Phi(X))=\\operatorname{Tr}(X)$ for every $X\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$. A **finite quantum channel** is a complex-linear map that is both completely positive and trace-preserving, abbreviated CPTP. Unitality, $\\Phi(I_d)=I_e$, is a separate condition and is not part of this definition.\n\nThis is a mathematical definition. A physical preparation, measurement, or open-system model must separately identify when one of these maps represents a physical transformation.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-complex-exponential-addition-and-real-extension",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For all $z,w\\in\\mathbb C$, $\\exp(z+w)=\\exp z\\exp w$. For real $x$, the complex value $\\exp(x+0i)$ equals the published real exponential $e^x$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[def-complex-exponential]], [[lem-complex-exponential-series-converges-everywhere]], [[lem-cauchy-product-of-absolutely-convergent-complex-series]], [[def-real-exponential-function-and-e]], [[lem-binomial-theorem-over-complex-numbers]], [[thm-binomial-closed-formula]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Write $E_{jk}=|j\\rangle\\langle k|$, $j,k\\in\\{0,1\\}$, so $I=E_{00}+E_{11}$. In this basis, $H=\\operatorname{diag}(0,\\hbar\\omega)$, $L=\\sqrt\\gamma E_{01}$, and $L^*L=\\gamma E_{11}$. Direct multiplication in [F1] gives $\\mathcal L(E_{00})=0$, $\\mathcal L(E_{11})=\\gamma(E_{00}-E_{11})$, $\\mathcal L(E_{01})=(i\\omega-\\gamma/2)E_{01}$, and $\\mathcal L(E_{10})=(-i\\omega-\\gamma/2)E_{10}$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "The matrix space is finite-dimensional and complete by [F2]. The trace-ideal estimate [F4] applied to the finitely many left and right multiplications in [F1] bounds $\\|\\mathcal L(X)\\|_1$ by a finite constant times $\\|X\\|_1$, so $\\mathcal L$ is bounded; [F2] therefore gives $e^{t\\mathcal L}=\\sum_{n\\geq0}t^n\\mathcal L^n/n!$. Put $D=E_{00}-E_{11}$. Step 1.1 gives $\\mathcal L(D)=-\\gamma D$ and $\\mathcal L(E_{11})=\\gamma D$; induction therefore gives $\\mathcal L^n(E_{11})=\\gamma(-\\gamma)^{n-1}D$ for $n\\geq1$. Summing this scalar series using [F3] and the real-extension clause [F7] yields $e^{t\\mathcal L}(E_{11})=E_{11}+(1-e^{-\\gamma t})D$, including $\\gamma=0$ because both the added coefficient and every positive power term then vanish. Also step 1.1 gives $\\mathcal L^n(E_{00})=0$ for $n\\geq1$, while the two coherence matrix units are eigenvectors, so [F3] gives $e^{t\\mathcal L}(E_{01})=e^{(i\\omega-\\gamma/2)t}E_{01}$ and $e^{t\\mathcal L}(E_{10})=e^{(-i\\omega-\\gamma/2)t}E_{10}$. Applying these four identities linearly to $\\rho(0)=\\sum_{j,k}\\rho_{jk}(0)E_{jk}$ gives all four formulas in the Statement.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F7",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "By [F5] and [F6], the full evolution map is completely positive and trace-preserving for $t\\geq0$, so it carries each initial density matrix to a density matrix. These formulas are deductions within the selected approximation and assert no microscopic bath limit.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The statement fixes a qubit and a single jump-operator family."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
