          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Work with finite-dimensional system and environment spaces $H_S=\\mathbb C^d$ and $H_E=\\mathbb C^m$, where $d,m\\geq1$, using the ordered tensor product $H_S\\otimes H_E$ with the system factor first. A system or environment state is a positive semidefinite Hermitian matrix of trace one; these state matrices are dimensionless. At the initial time, for every allowed system state $\\rho$, adopt the joint preparation $\\rho\\otimes\\eta$, where the fixed environment state $\\eta$ does not depend on $\\rho$. For each $t\\geq0$, choose a declared unitary $U_t$ on $H_S\\otimes H_E$, meaning $U_t^*U_t=U_tU_t^*=I$, with $U_0=I$. The reduced system dynamics is the map $\\Phi_t(\\rho)=\\operatorname{Tr}_E\\!\\left(U_t(\\rho\\otimes\\eta)U_t^*\\right)$, where for the fixed product basis the finite partial trace has entries $(\\operatorname{Tr}_E R)_{\\alpha\\beta}=\\sum_{j=1}^mR_{(\\alpha,j),(\\beta,j)}$. This assignment excludes initially correlated joint states. It imposes no one-parameter group, continuity, or semigroup law on $U_t$.\n\nA time-homogeneous finite GKSL model may instead be selected as a separate approximation: choose a fixed finite-dimensional Hamiltonian matrix $H=H^*$ in joules, a constant $\\hbar>0$ in joule-seconds, and finitely many jump matrices $L_a$ in $\\mathrm{s}^{-1/2}$, and use $T_t=e^{t\\mathcal L}$ for $t\\geq0$ with $\\mathcal L(X)=-\\frac{i}{\\hbar}[H,X]+\\sum_a\\left(L_aXL_a^*-\\frac12\\{L_a^*L_a,X\\}\\right)$. Each application must state its rates and physical regime. Selecting this approximation is not a consequence of the finite unitary reduction, and this postulate claims no weak-coupling or reservoir limit.",
