        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qsm-dt-gksl-definition",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a finite dimension $d\\geq1$, a positive constant $\\hbar$ in joule-seconds, a Hermitian matrix $H\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$ in joules, and a finite family $L_1,\\ldots,L_m\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$ with units ${\\rm s}^{-1/2}$, where $m\\geq0$. On dimensionless matrices $X\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$, define the **finite Gorini–Kossakowski–Sudarshan–Lindblad (GKSL) generator**\n\n$$\\mathcal L(X)=-\\frac{i}{\\hbar}[H,X]+\\sum_{a=1}^{m}\\left(L_aXL_a^*-\\frac12\\{L_a^*L_a,X\\}\\right),$$\n\nwhere $[A,B]=AB-BA$, $\\{A,B\\}=AB+BA$, and $A^*$ is the conjugate transpose. Each summand has units ${\\rm s}^{-1}$ times the units of $X$. An empty sum when $m=0$ is zero. The resulting $\\mathcal L$ is a linear map on the matrix space used in the finite-channel definition [[def-qsm-dt-channel-definition]].\n\nFor a compact time interval $I\\subset\\mathbb R$ whose coordinate is time in seconds, continuous functions $H:I\\to M_d(\\mathbb C)$ and $L_a:I\\to M_d(\\mathbb C)$ with $H(t)=H(t)^*$ define the corresponding **continuous finite GKSL family** $\\mathcal L_t$ by the same formula at each $t$. A propagator for this family means a family of linear maps $\\Lambda(t,s)$, $s\\leq t$ in $I$, satisfying $\\partial_t\\Lambda(t,s)=\\mathcal L_t\\circ\\Lambda(t,s)$ and $\\Lambda(s,s)=\\operatorname{id}$. This definition alone asserts neither existence nor uniqueness.\n\nThese are finite-dimensional mathematical definitions. They do not assert that a physical system follows this generator or that a microscopic bath has a Markovian limit.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-nrqmx-finite-jump-trajectories",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $d\\geq1$, let $r\\geq0$ be an integer, let $H=H^*\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$ have units joules, let $\\hbar>0$ have units joule-seconds, and let $L_1,\\ldots,L_r\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$ have units $\\mathrm{s}^{-1/2}$, with the finite GKSL convention of [[def-qsm-dt-gksl-definition]]. Put $K=-iH/\\hbar-\\frac12\\sum_{a=1}^rL_a^*L_a$ and $R(u)=e^{uK}$ for $u\\geq0$, with the exponential defined by its norm-convergent power series. For a density matrix $\\rho\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$, meaning $\\rho=\\rho^*\\geq0$ and $\\operatorname{Tr}\\rho=1$, define the no-jump branch at time $t\\geq0$ by $W_{\\varnothing,\\varnothing}(t)=R(t)$ and its branch state by $W_{\\varnothing,\\varnothing}(t)\\rho W_{\\varnothing,\\varnothing}(t)^*$.\n\nAll time variables $u,t,s_1,\\ldots,s_n$ are measured in seconds. For $n\\geq1$, a jump-history label is $\\mathbf a=(a_1,\\ldots,a_n)\\in\\{1,\\ldots,r\\}^n$ and its jump times are seconds $0<s_1<\\cdots<s_n<t$. Define the corresponding branch operator by $W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)=R(t-s_n)L_{a_n}R(s_n-s_{n-1})\\cdots L_{a_2}R(s_2-s_1)L_{a_1}R(s_1)$ and the unnormalized branch state by $W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)\\rho W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)^*$. Its branch-weight density is $w_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)=\\operatorname{Tr}(W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)\\rho W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)^*)$. The ordered-simplex integral of this density means the repeated Riemann integral $\\int_0^t ds_n\\int_0^{s_n}ds_{n-1}\\cdots\\int_0^{s_2}ds_1$, applied componentwise to matrices; each continuous real component is integrable on the compact integration interval. For $n\\geq1$, $W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}$ has units $\\mathrm{s}^{-n/2}$ and $w_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}$ has units $\\mathrm{s}^{-n}$; integrating over the $n$ time variables gives a dimensionless weight. If $r=0$, there are no branches with $n\\geq1$. This definition alone does not assert that the branch weights sum to one.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "cor-finite-dimensional-normed-spaces-are-banach",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a normed space over $\\mathbb K\\in\\{\\mathbb R,\\mathbb C\\}$ and assume\n$X$ admits an ordered basis of finite length. Then $X$ is a Banach space in the\nsense of [[def-banach-space]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-exponential-series-of-a-bounded-operator",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a real or complex Banach space and let $A\\in\\mathcal B(X)$ ([[def-bounded-linear-operator]]). For $t\\in\\mathbb R$ define $E(t):=\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty\\frac{t^n}{n!}A^n$. Then the series converges absolutely in the operator norm $\\|\\cdot\\|$ of $\\mathcal B(X)$, uniformly for $t$ in compact subsets of $\\mathbb R$; $E(t)\\in\\mathcal B(X)$ with $\\|E(t)\\| \\le e^{|t|\\|A\\|}$; $E(0)=I$ and $E(t+s)=E(t)E(s)$ for all $s,t\\in\\mathbb R$; $t\\mapsto E(t)$ is of class $C^\\infty$ in the operator norm with $$E'(t)=AE(t)=E(t)A,$$ and $\\|E(t)-I-tA\\| \\le\\frac{t^2}{2}\\|A\\|^2e^{|t|\\|A\\|}$, so that $\\frac{E(t)-I}{t}\\to A$ in operator norm as $t\\to0$. In particular $E$ is a uniformly continuous (hence strongly continuous) group of bounded operators on $X$ whose generator is the bounded operator $A$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-qsm-dt-finite-matrix-trace-ideal",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal H$ be a complex Hilbert space of finite dimension $d\\ge1$. For a matrix $X$ define $|X|=(X^*X)^{1/2}$ by finite Hermitian functional calculus and define its trace norm by $\\|X\\|_1=\\operatorname{Tr}|X|$. This is a complete norm on the finite-dimensional matrix space. Write $\\|Y\\|:=\\sup\\{\\|Yv\\|:\\|v\\|\\le1\\}$ for the operator norm induced by the Hilbert-space norm. For all matrices $X,Y$,\n$$\n\\|YX\\|_1\\le\\|Y\\|\\|X\\|_1,\\qquad \\|XY\\|_1\\le\\|Y\\|\\|X\\|_1,\\qquad |\\operatorname{Tr}X|\\le\\|X\\|_1.\n$$\nFor unitary $U$, $\\|UXU^*\\|_1=\\|X\\|_1$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-qsm-dt-gksl-sufficient",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal L$ be a finite GKSL generator on $M_d(\\mathbb C)$, $d\\geq1$, with the units and conventions of [[def-qsm-dt-gksl-definition]]. For a bounded operator $A$ on a complex Banach space, write $e^{tA}:=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}t^nA^n/n!$ for the operator-norm convergent series of [[lem-exponential-series-of-a-bounded-operator]]. Then $T_t=e^{t\\mathcal L}$ is a finite channel for every $t\\geq0$. The family satisfies $T_0=\\operatorname{id}$, $T_{t+s}=T_t\\circ T_s$, and is norm-continuous. Time is measured in seconds; no negative-time channel or physical bath limit is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-operator-norm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$ and $Y$ be normed spaces over the same scalar field, and let\n$T:X\\to Y$ be bounded in the sense of [[def-bounded-linear-operator]]. The\n**operator norm** of $T$ is\n\n$$\\|T\\| := \\sup\\{\\|Tx\\| : x \\in X,\\ \\|x\\| \\le 1\\}.$$\n\nThe set on the right contains $0$ and every bound $C$ for $T$ bounds it\nabove. Thus its finite real supremum exists by the proved least-upper-bound\nproperty of the library's real field ([[cor-cauchy-reals-lub-complete]]).\nIn particular every number strictly below the supremum is exceeded by a\nmember of this set; otherwise it would be a smaller upper bound. No choice\nprinciple is used for this assertion.\n\nThe same number is the least bound of $T$:\n\n$$\\|T\\| = \\inf\\{C \\ge 0 : \\|Tx\\| \\le C\\|x\\| \\text{ for all } x \\in X\\}.$$\n\nIf $X \\ne \\{0\\}$, positive homogeneity also gives\n\n$$\\|T\\| = \\sup\\{\\|Tx\\| : \\|x\\| = 1\\}.$$\n\nWhen $X = \\{0\\}$, the unit sphere is empty and the unit-ball supremum is $0$,\nso $\\|T\\| = 0$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "def-darboux-integral",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $a < b$ be reals and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be bounded\n([[def-bounded-set]]). Write $\\mathcal{P}$ for the set of all partitions of\n$[a,b]$ ([[def-partition-and-refinement]]) and put\n\n$$\\mathcal{L} \\;:=\\; \\{\\, L(f,P) \\ : \\ P \\in \\mathcal{P} \\,\\}, \\qquad \\mathcal{U} \\;:=\\; \\{\\, U(f,P) \\ : \\ P \\in \\mathcal{P} \\,\\}$$\n\nfor the sets of lower and of upper Darboux sums ([[def-darboux-sums]]).\n\n### Both extrema exist\n\n**$\\mathcal{P}$ is nonempty**: the pair $(1, t)$ with $t_0 := a$ and $t_k := b$\nfor $k \\ge 1$ is a partition of $[a,b]$, since $a < b$. So $\\mathcal{L}$ and\n$\\mathcal{U}$ are nonempty.\n\n**$\\mathcal{L}$ is bounded above and $\\mathcal{U}$ is bounded below.** Fix any\n$Q \\in \\mathcal{P}$. By claim 2 of [[lem-refinement-inequalities]],\n$L(f,P) \\le U(f,Q)$ for every $P \\in \\mathcal{P}$, so $U(f,Q)$ is an upper bound\nof $\\mathcal{L}$; and $L(f,Q) \\le U(f,P)$ for every $P$, so $L(f,Q)$ is a lower\nbound of $\\mathcal{U}$.\n\nHence a nonempty set bounded above has a supremum\n([[def-complete-ordered-field]]) and a nonempty set bounded below has an infimum\n([[thm-infimum-property]], [[def-infimum]]), each unique ([[lem-sup-unique]]).\nThe **lower** and **upper Darboux integrals** of $f$ over $[a,b]$ are the real\nnumbers\n\n$$\\underline{\\int_a^b} f \\;:=\\; \\sup \\mathcal{L} \\;=\\; \\sup_{P} L(f,P), \\qquad \\overline{\\int_a^b} f \\;:=\\; \\inf \\mathcal{U} \\;=\\; \\inf_{P} U(f,P) .$$\n\n### The lower integral never exceeds the upper one\n\n$$\\underline{\\int_a^b} f \\;\\le\\; \\overline{\\int_a^b} f .$$\n\nIndeed, for each fixed $Q \\in \\mathcal{P}$ the number $U(f,Q)$ is an upper bound\nof $\\mathcal{L}$, so the least upper bound satisfies\n$\\underline{\\int_a^b} f \\le U(f,Q)$. As $Q$ was arbitrary, $\\underline{\\int_a^b}f$\nis a lower bound of $\\mathcal{U}$, and the greatest lower bound satisfies\n$\\underline{\\int_a^b} f \\le \\overline{\\int_a^b} f$ ([[def-infimum]]).\n\nMoreover, for **every** partition $P$,\n\n$$L(f,P) \\;\\le\\; \\underline{\\int_a^b} f \\;\\le\\; \\overline{\\int_a^b} f \\;\\le\\; U(f,P) ,$$\n\nthe outer inequalities because a member of a set is at most its supremum and at\nleast its infimum.\n\n### Integrability\n\n$f$ is **Darboux integrable on $[a,b]$**, and on this page simply\n**integrable**, when\n\n$$\\underline{\\int_a^b} f \\;=\\; \\overline{\\int_a^b} f ,$$\n\nand then the common value is written\n\n$$\\int_a^b f \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad \\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x ,$$\n\nthe **integral of $f$ over $[a,b]$**. It is a single well-determined real\nnumber, being the common value of two numbers each of which is unique\n([[lem-sup-unique]]). Without the displayed equality the symbol $\\int_a^b f$ is\nnot defined and is never written.\n\n**The inequality above is the whole difficulty.** By the previous paragraph\nintegrability is *never* a question of one integral exceeding the other, only of\nthe gap $\\overline{\\int_a^b} f - \\underline{\\int_a^b} f \\ge 0$ being $0$; and by\n[[thm-riemann-criterion]] that gap is $0$ exactly when a single partition can be\nfound making $U(f,P) - L(f,P)$ small. Whether that is possible is settled\ncompletely, in terms of the discontinuities of $f$, by\n[[thm-lebesgue-criterion]].\n\n**\"Riemann integrable\" means the same thing here.** The definition above is\nDarboux's. Riemann's own definition, in terms of tagged partitions of small\nmesh, is [[def-tagged-partition-and-riemann-sum]], and the two define the same\nclass of functions with the same integral by [[thm-darboux-equals-riemann]].\nUntil that theorem is proved the two phrases are kept apart; after it they are\nused interchangeably, as they are throughout the literature.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-continuous-implies-integrable",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a < b$ be reals and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be continuous on $[a,b]$\n(Continuity of $f : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ at a point of $A$ and on $A$: the $\\varepsilon$-$\\delta$ condition, its agreement with $\\lim_{x \\to c} f(x) = f(c)$ at a limit point, and continuity at an isolated point). Then $f$ is bounded (Lower bound, bounded below, bounded set) and Riemann\nintegrable on $[a,b]$ (The lower and upper Darboux integrals of a bounded $f$ on $[a,b]$ as $\\sup_P L(f,P)$ and $\\inf_P U(f,P)$, Darboux integrability as their equality, and the notation $\\int_a^b f$).\n\n**The proof gives more than integrability: it gives a partition that works.** For\nevery real $\\varepsilon > 0$ the uniform partition into $N$ parts already\nsatisfies $U(f,P) - L(f,P) < \\varepsilon$, as soon as $N$ is large enough that\n$(b-a)/\\iota(N)$ is below the $\\delta$ that uniform continuity supplies for\n$\\varepsilon/\\bigl(2(b-a)\\bigr)$. Uniform continuity is exactly what makes one\n$\\delta$ serve all $N$ subintervals at once, and it is the only place where the\ncompactness of $[a,b]$ is used.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a<b$. Suppose $G:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b)$. If $f:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb R$ is Riemann integrable and\n\n$$f(x)=G'(x)\\qquad(a<x<b),$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\\int_a^b f=G(b)-G(a).$$\n\nNo derivative of $G$ at either endpoint is assumed, and the two endpoint values assigned to the integrable extension $f$ do not enter the conclusion.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "lem-derivative-of-a-power",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Powers are those of Integer powers $a^m$, and $\\iota : \\mathbb{N} \\to\n\\mathbb{R}$ is the canonical natural of The canonical natural $\\iota(n) = n \\cdot 1_F$ of a field, so that\n$\\iota(0) = 0$ and $\\iota(m+1) = \\iota(m) + 1$. Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\n1. The function $p_0 : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$, $p_0(x) = x^{0}$, is the\n   constant function $1$, and it is differentiable at every $c \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n   with $p_0'(c) = 0$ (The derivative $f'(c) = \\lim_{x \\to c} \\frac{f(x) - f(c)}{x - c}$ of $f : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ at a point $c \\in A$ that is a limit point of $A$, and differentiability on a set).\n2. For $n \\ge 1$ the function $p_n : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$,\n   $p_n(x) = x^{n}$, is differentiable at every $c \\in \\mathbb{R}$, and\n   $$p_n'(c) \\;=\\; \\iota(n)\\,c^{\\,n-1} .$$\n3. For $n \\ge 1$ put $A_0 := \\{\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R} : x \\ne 0 \\,\\}$. The function\n   $m_n : A_0 \\to \\mathbb{R}$, $m_n(x) = x^{-n}$, is differentiable at every\n   $c \\in A_0$ as a function on $A_0$, and\n   $$m_n'(c) \\;=\\; -\\,\\iota(n)\\,c^{\\,-n-1} .$$\n4. Let $a : \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with $a_j = 0$ for $j \\ge n$, and let\n   $P : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be the polynomial function\n   $P(x) = \\sum_{j<n} a_j x^{j}$ (Finite sums and finite products, by recursion). Then $P$ is\n   differentiable at every $c \\in \\mathbb{R}$, and, defining $e : \\mathbb{N}\n   \\to \\mathbb{R}$ by $e_0 := 0$ and $e_j := \\iota(j)\\,a_j\\,c^{\\,j-1}$ for\n   $j \\ge 1$,\n   $$P'(c) \\;=\\; \\sum_{j<n} e_j .$$\n\n**Claim 2 is stated for $n \\ge 1$ and not for $n \\ge 0$, and that is not\ntimidity.** At $n = 0$ its right-hand side reads $\\iota(0)\\,c^{-1}$, and\n$c^{-1}$ is not defined at $c = 0$ (Integer powers $a^m$), so the formula is\nnot a statement about the whole line. Claim 1 is what covers $n = 0$, and it\nsays the derivative is $0$ there, which is what the informal reading\n\"$0 \\cdot c^{-1}$\" is reaching for. The same shift is why the term $e_0$ of\nclaim 4 is defined to be $0$ outright rather than by the formula.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Under [A1], put $D=\\sum_aL_a^*L_a$ and $K=-iH/\\hbar-D/2$. Then $K+K^*=-D$. The matrix space is complete by [F3], so [F4] differentiates $R(u)=e^{uK}$. For every vector $v$, $\\frac{d}{du}\\|R(u)v\\|^2=\\langle(K+K^*)R(u)v,R(u)v\\rangle=-\\sum_a\\|L_aR(u)v\\|^2\\leq0$. Since $R(0)=I$, the continuous derivative is integrable by [F8], and [F9] gives $\\|R(u)v\\|\\leq\\|v\\|$ for $u\\geq0$, hence $R(u)$ is a contraction.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F8",
            "F9",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "On $M_d(\\mathbb C)$ define $\\mathcal N(X)=KX+XK^*$, $\\mathcal J(X)=\\sum_aL_aXL_a^*$, and $\\mathcal T_u=e^{u\\mathcal L}$. By [F5], these finite sums of left and right matrix multiplications are bounded in trace norm. Expanding [F1] gives $\\mathcal L=\\mathcal N+\\mathcal J$. Fix $t>0$. Integrate finite-dimensional matrix and superoperator entries by their real and imaginary components, using the repeated Riemann convention in [F2]. The curve $s\\mapsto e^{(t-s)\\mathcal N}\\mathcal T_s$ has continuously differentiable entries by [F4]; its real and imaginary derivative components are continuous and hence integrable by [F8]. Differentiation gives $\\frac{d}{ds}(e^{(t-s)\\mathcal N}\\mathcal T_s)=e^{(t-s)\\mathcal N}\\mathcal J\\mathcal T_s$, since $\\mathcal L=\\mathcal N+\\mathcal J$. Applying [F9] entrywise yields $\\mathcal T_t=e^{t\\mathcal N}+\\int_0^t e^{(t-s)\\mathcal N}\\mathcal J\\mathcal T_s\\,ds$. This finite identity follows by induction on $N$: the base case is the displayed variation-of-constants identity, and substituting that identity for the final $\\mathcal T_{s_1}$ in the remainder adds the next ordered-simplex term and a remainder with one more nested time. Thus, for each $N\\geq0$, $\\mathcal T_t=\\sum_{n=0}^{N}S_n(t)+Q_{N+1}(t)$, where $S_0(t)=e^{t\\mathcal N}$, $S_n(t)=\\int_{0<s_1<\\cdots<s_n<t}e^{(t-s_n)\\mathcal N}\\mathcal J e^{(s_n-s_{n-1})\\mathcal N}\\cdots\\mathcal J e^{s_1\\mathcal N}\\,d\\mathbf s$ for $n\\geq1$, and $Q_{N+1}(t)=\\int_{0<s_1<\\cdots<s_{N+1}<t}e^{(t-s_{N+1})\\mathcal N}\\mathcal J\\cdots\\mathcal J e^{s_1\\mathcal N}\\mathcal T_{s_1}\\,d\\mathbf s$. These repeated componentwise Riemann integrals exist because their finite-dimensional integrands are continuous.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F8",
            "F9",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Left multiplication by $K$ and right multiplication by $K^*$ commute; multiplying their absolutely norm-convergent exponential series and grouping by total degree with the binomial formula yields $e^{u\\mathcal N}(X)=e^{uK}Xe^{uK^*}=R(u)XR(u)^*$. This establishes the no-jump superoperator formula used in the norm estimates below.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "From [F5] and [F7], $\\|\\mathcal J(X)\\|_1\\leq(\\sum_a\\|L_a\\|^2)\\|X\\|_1$ and $\\|\\mathcal N(X)\\|_1\\leq2\\|K\\|\\|X\\|_1$, so the induced $1\\to1$ norms are finite. By step 2.1 and the contraction in step 1.1, [F5] gives $\\|e^{u\\mathcal N}\\|_{1\\to1}\\leq1$ for $u\\geq0$. The ordered-simplex volume is $t^n/n!$: the zero-variable volume is $1$; if the $n$-variable volume is $s^n/n!$, then [F10] gives the primitive $s^{n+1}/(n+1)!$, and [F8] makes the integrand Riemann integrable, so [F9] evaluates the next repeated integral as $t^{n+1}/(n+1)!$. Induction proves the volume formula. Thus $\\|S_n(t)\\|_{1\\to1}\\leq(t\\|\\mathcal J\\|_{1\\to1})^n/n!$. By [F4], $\\|\\mathcal T_s\\|_{1\\to1}\\leq e^{s\\|\\mathcal L\\|_{1\\to1}}$, so $\\|Q_{N+1}(t)\\|_{1\\to1}\\leq e^{t\\|\\mathcal L\\|_{1\\to1}}(t\\|\\mathcal J\\|_{1\\to1})^{N+1}/(N+1)!\\to0$. Replacing $t$ by any bound $T$ gives summable bounds for all $0\\leq t\\leq T$ and a remainder tending uniformly to zero. Thus the series $\\sum_n S_n(t)$ converges in induced trace norm to $\\mathcal T_t$, uniformly on bounded time intervals.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "F9",
            "F10",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "If $t=0$, then $\\mathcal T_0=\\operatorname{id}$ and $R(0)=I$; all positive-jump simplexes are empty, and the sole branch has weight $\\operatorname{Tr}\\rho=1$. For $t>0$, applying the operator identity from step 3.1 to $\\rho$ and expanding each occurrence of $\\mathcal J$ in $S_n(t)$ sums over labels $a_1,\\ldots,a_n$. Step 2.1 turns each factor $e^{u\\mathcal N}$ into the no-jump conjugation, so each integrand is $W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)\\rho W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)^*$ by [F2]. This matrix is positive since $\\langle W\\rho W^*v,v\\rangle=\\langle\\rho W^*v,W^*v\\rangle\\geq0$ for every vector $v$; this argument uses only $\\rho\\geq0$, proving branch positivity for all positive inputs. Trace-norm convergence from step 3.1 and [F5] allow taking traces term by term. Each integrated branch weight is nonnegative: its continuous trace integrand is nonnegative pointwise, so every Darboux lower sum, and therefore its integral, is nonnegative by [F8]. By [F6], $\\operatorname{Tr}(e^{t\\mathcal L}\\rho)=\\operatorname{Tr}\\rho=1$, so the nonnegative integrated branch weights sum to one. If $r=0$, then $\\mathcal J=0$, all $S_n$ with $n\\geq1$ vanish, and only the no-jump term remains.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F8",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 4.1 treats r=0: J=0 and all positive-jump terms vanish."
