      "finite_checks_disposition": "Exact finite matrix and boundary substitutions are recorded; these check the displayed finite examples and supplement the symbolic proof.",
      "supplier_excerpts": [
        {
          "supplier": "post-nrqmx-finite-open-model",
          "carrier": "items/post-nrqmx-finite-open-model.md",
          "status": "Current carrier status: draft; inspected for this dependency interface.",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "exact_statement": "Work with finite-dimensional system and environment spaces $H_S=\\mathbb C^d$ and $H_E=\\mathbb C^m$, where $d,m\\geq1$, using the ordered tensor product $H_S\\otimes H_E$ with the system factor first. A system or environment state is a positive semidefinite Hermitian matrix of trace one; these state matrices are dimensionless. At the initial time, for every allowed system state $\\rho$, adopt the joint preparation $\\rho\\otimes\\eta$, where the fixed environment state $\\eta$ does not depend on $\\rho$. For each $t\\geq0$, choose a declared unitary $U_t$ on $H_S\\otimes H_E$, meaning $U_t^*U_t=U_tU_t^*=I$, with $U_0=I$. The reduced system dynamics is the map $\\Phi_t(\\rho)=\\operatorname{Tr}_E\\!\\left(U_t(\\rho\\otimes\\eta)U_t^*\\right)$, where for the fixed product basis the finite partial trace has entries $(\\operatorname{Tr}_E R)_{\\alpha\\beta}=\\sum_{j=1}^mR_{(\\alpha,j),(\\beta,j)}$. This assignment excludes initially correlated joint states. It imposes no one-parameter group, continuity, or semigroup law on $U_t$.\n\nA time-homogeneous finite GKSL model may instead be selected as a separate approximation: choose a fixed finite-dimensional Hamiltonian matrix $H=H^*$ in joules, a constant $\\hbar>0$ in joule-seconds, and finitely many jump matrices $L_a$ in $\\mathrm{s}^{-1/2}$, and use $T_t=e^{t\\mathcal L}$ for $t\\geq0$ with $\\mathcal L(X)=-\\frac{i}{\\hbar}[H,X]+\\sum_a\\left(L_aXL_a^*-\\frac12\\{L_a^*L_a,X\\}\\right)$. Each application must state its rates and physical regime. Selecting this approximation is not a consequence of the finite unitary reduction, and this postulate claims no weak-coupling or reservoir limit.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Applicable consumer restatements: A1: [A1] [[post-nrqmx-finite-open-model]]: the time-homogeneous finite GKSL family is a separately selected approximation within the open-system model; it is not derived from the exact finite unitary reduction.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier status is draft; the exact current interface was inspected; proof is not applicable to this carrier.",
          "consumer_use": "A1 is used in proof steps 1.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-nrqmx-finite-jump-trajectories",
          "carrier": "items/def-nrqmx-finite-jump-trajectories.md",
          "status": "Current carrier status: draft; inspected for this dependency interface.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $d\\geq1$, let $r\\geq0$ be an integer, let $H=H^*\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$ have units joules, let $\\hbar>0$ have units joule-seconds, and let $L_1,\\ldots,L_r\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$ have units $\\mathrm{s}^{-1/2}$, with the finite GKSL convention of [[def-qsm-dt-gksl-definition]]. Put $K=-iH/\\hbar-\\frac12\\sum_{a=1}^rL_a^*L_a$ and $R(u)=e^{uK}$ for $u\\geq0$, with the exponential defined by its norm-convergent power series. For a density matrix $\\rho\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$, meaning $\\rho=\\rho^*\\geq0$ and $\\operatorname{Tr}\\rho=1$, define the no-jump branch at time $t\\geq0$ by $W_{\\varnothing,\\varnothing}(t)=R(t)$ and its branch state by $W_{\\varnothing,\\varnothing}(t)\\rho W_{\\varnothing,\\varnothing}(t)^*$.\n\nAll time variables $u,t,s_1,\\ldots,s_n$ are measured in seconds. For $n\\geq1$, a jump-history label is $\\mathbf a=(a_1,\\ldots,a_n)\\in\\{1,\\ldots,r\\}^n$ and its jump times are seconds $0<s_1<\\cdots<s_n<t$. Define the corresponding branch operator by $W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)=R(t-s_n)L_{a_n}R(s_n-s_{n-1})\\cdots L_{a_2}R(s_2-s_1)L_{a_1}R(s_1)$ and the unnormalized branch state by $W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)\\rho W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)^*$. Its branch-weight density is $w_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)=\\operatorname{Tr}(W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)\\rho W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)^*)$. The ordered-simplex integral of this density means the repeated Riemann integral $\\int_0^t ds_n\\int_0^{s_n}ds_{n-1}\\cdots\\int_0^{s_2}ds_1$, applied componentwise to matrices; each continuous real component is integrable on the compact integration interval. For $n\\geq1$, $W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}$ has units $\\mathrm{s}^{-n/2}$ and $w_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}$ has units $\\mathrm{s}^{-n}$; integrating over the $n$ time variables gives a dimensionless weight. If $r=0$, there are no branches with $n\\geq1$. This definition alone does not assert that the branch weights sum to one.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Applicable consumer restatements: F2: [F2] [[def-nrqmx-finite-jump-trajectories]]: $K=-iH/\\hbar-\\frac12\\sum_aL_a^*L_a$, $R(u)=e^{uK}$, and $W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)$ is the ordered product of no-jump factors and jump matrices; the branch state is $W\\rho W^*$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier status is draft; the exact current interface was inspected; proof is not applicable to this carrier.",
          "consumer_use": "F2 is used in proof steps 1.1, 1.2, 2.1, 3.1, 4.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-qsm-dt-gksl-definition",
          "carrier": "items/def-qsm-dt-gksl-definition.md",
          "status": "Current carrier status: published; inspected for this dependency interface.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Fix a finite dimension $d\\geq1$, a positive constant $\\hbar$ in joule-seconds, a Hermitian matrix $H\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$ in joules, and a finite family $L_1,\\ldots,L_m\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$ with units ${\\rm s}^{-1/2}$, where $m\\geq0$. On dimensionless matrices $X\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$, define the **finite Gorini–Kossakowski–Sudarshan–Lindblad (GKSL) generator**\n\n$$\\mathcal L(X)=-\\frac{i}{\\hbar}[H,X]+\\sum_{a=1}^{m}\\left(L_aXL_a^*-\\frac12\\{L_a^*L_a,X\\}\\right),$$\n\nwhere $[A,B]=AB-BA$, $\\{A,B\\}=AB+BA$, and $A^*$ is the conjugate transpose. Each summand has units ${\\rm s}^{-1}$ times the units of $X$. An empty sum when $m=0$ is zero. The resulting $\\mathcal L$ is a linear map on the matrix space used in the finite-channel definition [[def-qsm-dt-channel-definition]].\n\nFor a compact time interval $I\\subset\\mathbb R$ whose coordinate is time in seconds, continuous functions $H:I\\to M_d(\\mathbb C)$ and $L_a:I\\to M_d(\\mathbb C)$ with $H(t)=H(t)^*$ define the corresponding **continuous finite GKSL family** $\\mathcal L_t$ by the same formula at each $t$. A propagator for this family means a family of linear maps $\\Lambda(t,s)$, $s\\leq t$ in $I$, satisfying $\\partial_t\\Lambda(t,s)=\\mathcal L_t\\circ\\Lambda(t,s)$ and $\\Lambda(s,s)=\\operatorname{id}$. This definition alone asserts neither existence nor uniqueness.\n\nThese are finite-dimensional mathematical definitions. They do not assert that a physical system follows this generator or that a microscopic bath has a Markovian limit.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Applicable consumer restatements: F1: [F1] [[def-qsm-dt-gksl-definition]]: $\\mathcal L(X)=-\\frac{i}{\\hbar}[H,X]+\\sum_a(L_aXL_a^*-\\frac12\\{L_a^*L_a,X\\})$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier status is published; this carrier has no proof section, as appropriate to its kind. No independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F1 is used in proof steps 1.1, 1.2"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-qsm-dt-gksl-sufficient",
          "carrier": "items/thm-qsm-dt-gksl-sufficient.md",
          "status": "Current carrier status: published; inspected for this dependency interface.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $\\mathcal L$ be a finite GKSL generator on $M_d(\\mathbb C)$, $d\\geq1$, with the units and conventions of [[def-qsm-dt-gksl-definition]]. For a bounded operator $A$ on a complex Banach space, write $e^{tA}:=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}t^nA^n/n!$ for the operator-norm convergent series of [[lem-exponential-series-of-a-bounded-operator]]. Then $T_t=e^{t\\mathcal L}$ is a finite channel for every $t\\geq0$. The family satisfies $T_0=\\operatorname{id}$, $T_{t+s}=T_t\\circ T_s$, and is norm-continuous. Time is measured in seconds; no negative-time channel or physical bath limit is asserted.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Applicable consumer restatements: F6: [F6] [[thm-qsm-dt-gksl-sufficient]]: $e^{t\\mathcal L}$ is a trace-preserving finite channel for $t\\geq0$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier status is published; this carrier has no proof section, as appropriate to its kind. No independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F6 is used in proof steps 4.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-qsm-dt-finite-matrix-trace-ideal",
          "carrier": "items/lem-qsm-dt-finite-matrix-trace-ideal.md",
          "status": "Current carrier status: published; inspected for this dependency interface.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $\\mathcal H$ be a complex Hilbert space of finite dimension $d\\ge1$. For a matrix $X$ define $|X|=(X^*X)^{1/2}$ by finite Hermitian functional calculus and define its trace norm by $\\|X\\|_1=\\operatorname{Tr}|X|$. This is a complete norm on the finite-dimensional matrix space. Write $\\|Y\\|:=\\sup\\{\\|Yv\\|:\\|v\\|\\le1\\}$ for the operator norm induced by the Hilbert-space norm. For all matrices $X,Y$,\n$$\n\\|YX\\|_1\\le\\|Y\\|\\|X\\|_1,\\qquad \\|XY\\|_1\\le\\|Y\\|\\|X\\|_1,\\qquad |\\operatorname{Tr}X|\\le\\|X\\|_1.\n$$\nFor unitary $U$, $\\|UXU^*\\|_1=\\|X\\|_1$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Applicable consumer restatements: F5: [F5] [[lem-qsm-dt-finite-matrix-trace-ideal]]: the finite matrix trace norm is complete, satisfies $\\|AXB\\|_1\\leq\\|A\\|\\,\\|X\\|_1\\,\\|B\\|$, and $|\\operatorname{Tr}X|\\leq\\|X\\|_1$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier status is published; this carrier has no proof section, as appropriate to its kind. No independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F5 is used in proof steps 1.2, 3.1, 4.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "cor-finite-dimensional-normed-spaces-are-banach",
          "carrier": "items/cor-finite-dimensional-normed-spaces-are-banach.md",
          "status": "Current carrier status: published; inspected for this dependency interface.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $X$ be a normed space over $\\mathbb K\\in\\{\\mathbb R,\\mathbb C\\}$ and assume\n$X$ admits an ordered basis of finite length. Then $X$ is a Banach space in the\nsense of [[def-banach-space]].",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Applicable consumer restatements: F3: [F3] [[cor-finite-dimensional-normed-spaces-are-banach]]: every finite-dimensional normed matrix space is complete.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier status is published; this carrier has no proof section, as appropriate to its kind. No independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F3 is used in proof steps 1.1, 2.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-exponential-series-of-a-bounded-operator",
          "carrier": "items/lem-exponential-series-of-a-bounded-operator.md",
          "status": "Current carrier status: published; inspected for this dependency interface.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $X$ be a real or complex Banach space and let $A\\in\\mathcal B(X)$ ([[def-bounded-linear-operator]]). For $t\\in\\mathbb R$ define $E(t):=\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty\\frac{t^n}{n!}A^n$. Then the series converges absolutely in the operator norm $\\|\\cdot\\|$ of $\\mathcal B(X)$, uniformly for $t$ in compact subsets of $\\mathbb R$; $E(t)\\in\\mathcal B(X)$ with $\\|E(t)\\| \\le e^{|t|\\|A\\|}$; $E(0)=I$ and $E(t+s)=E(t)E(s)$ for all $s,t\\in\\mathbb R$; $t\\mapsto E(t)$ is of class $C^\\infty$ in the operator norm with $$E'(t)=AE(t)=E(t)A,$$ and $\\|E(t)-I-tA\\| \\le\\frac{t^2}{2}\\|A\\|^2e^{|t|\\|A\\|}$, so that $\\frac{E(t)-I}{t}\\to A$ in operator norm as $t\\to0$. In particular $E$ is a uniformly continuous (hence strongly continuous) group of bounded operators on $X$ whose generator is the bounded operator $A$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Applicable consumer restatements: F4: [F4] [[lem-exponential-series-of-a-bounded-operator]]: for a bounded operator $A$ on a Banach space, $e^{uA}$ is norm-continuous, differentiable with derivative $Ae^{uA}$, and satisfies $\\|e^{uA}\\|\\leq e^{|u|\\|A\\|}$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier status is published; this carrier has no proof section, as appropriate to its kind. No independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F4 is used in proof steps 1.1, 1.2, 2.1, 3.1, 4.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-operator-norm",
          "carrier": "items/def-operator-norm.md",
          "status": "Current carrier status: published; inspected for this dependency interface.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $X$ and $Y$ be normed spaces over the same scalar field, and let\n$T:X\\to Y$ be bounded in the sense of [[def-bounded-linear-operator]]. The\n**operator norm** of $T$ is\n\n$$\\|T\\| := \\sup\\{\\|Tx\\| : x \\in X,\\ \\|x\\| \\le 1\\}.$$\n\nThe set on the right contains $0$ and every bound $C$ for $T$ bounds it\nabove. Thus its finite real supremum exists by the proved least-upper-bound\nproperty of the library's real field ([[cor-cauchy-reals-lub-complete]]).\nIn particular every number strictly below the supremum is exceeded by a\nmember of this set; otherwise it would be a smaller upper bound. No choice\nprinciple is used for this assertion.\n\nThe same number is the least bound of $T$:\n\n$$\\|T\\| = \\inf\\{C \\ge 0 : \\|Tx\\| \\le C\\|x\\| \\text{ for all } x \\in X\\}.$$\n\nIf $X \\ne \\{0\\}$, positive homogeneity also gives\n\n$$\\|T\\| = \\sup\\{\\|Tx\\| : \\|x\\| = 1\\}.$$\n\nWhen $X = \\{0\\}$, the unit sphere is empty and the unit-ball supremum is $0$,\nso $\\|T\\| = 0$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Applicable consumer restatements: F7: [F7] [[def-operator-norm]]: for a bounded linear map between normed spaces, its operator norm is the supremum of the output norm over the input unit ball; here $\\|\\mathcal A\\|_{1\\to1}:=\\sup_{\\|X\\|_1\\leq1}\\|\\mathcal A(X)\\|_1$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier status is published; this carrier has no proof section, as appropriate to its kind. No independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F7 is used in proof steps 3.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-darboux-integral",
          "carrier": "items/def-darboux-integral.md",
          "status": "Current carrier status: published; inspected for this dependency interface.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $a < b$ be reals and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be bounded\n([[def-bounded-set]]). Write $\\mathcal{P}$ for the set of all partitions of\n$[a,b]$ ([[def-partition-and-refinement]]) and put\n\n$$\\mathcal{L} \\;:=\\; \\{\\, L(f,P) \\ : \\ P \\in \\mathcal{P} \\,\\}, \\qquad \\mathcal{U} \\;:=\\; \\{\\, U(f,P) \\ : \\ P \\in \\mathcal{P} \\,\\}$$\n\nfor the sets of lower and of upper Darboux sums ([[def-darboux-sums]]).\n\n### Both extrema exist\n\n**$\\mathcal{P}$ is nonempty**: the pair $(1, t)$ with $t_0 := a$ and $t_k := b$\nfor $k \\ge 1$ is a partition of $[a,b]$, since $a < b$. So $\\mathcal{L}$ and\n$\\mathcal{U}$ are nonempty.\n\n**$\\mathcal{L}$ is bounded above and $\\mathcal{U}$ is bounded below.** Fix any\n$Q \\in \\mathcal{P}$. By claim 2 of [[lem-refinement-inequalities]],\n$L(f,P) \\le U(f,Q)$ for every $P \\in \\mathcal{P}$, so $U(f,Q)$ is an upper bound\nof $\\mathcal{L}$; and $L(f,Q) \\le U(f,P)$ for every $P$, so $L(f,Q)$ is a lower\nbound of $\\mathcal{U}$.\n\nHence a nonempty set bounded above has a supremum\n([[def-complete-ordered-field]]) and a nonempty set bounded below has an infimum\n([[thm-infimum-property]], [[def-infimum]]), each unique ([[lem-sup-unique]]).\nThe **lower** and **upper Darboux integrals** of $f$ over $[a,b]$ are the real\nnumbers\n\n$$\\underline{\\int_a^b} f \\;:=\\; \\sup \\mathcal{L} \\;=\\; \\sup_{P} L(f,P), \\qquad \\overline{\\int_a^b} f \\;:=\\; \\inf \\mathcal{U} \\;=\\; \\inf_{P} U(f,P) .$$\n\n### The lower integral never exceeds the upper one\n\n$$\\underline{\\int_a^b} f \\;\\le\\; \\overline{\\int_a^b} f .$$\n\nIndeed, for each fixed $Q \\in \\mathcal{P}$ the number $U(f,Q)$ is an upper bound\nof $\\mathcal{L}$, so the least upper bound satisfies\n$\\underline{\\int_a^b} f \\le U(f,Q)$. As $Q$ was arbitrary, $\\underline{\\int_a^b}f$\nis a lower bound of $\\mathcal{U}$, and the greatest lower bound satisfies\n$\\underline{\\int_a^b} f \\le \\overline{\\int_a^b} f$ ([[def-infimum]]).\n\nMoreover, for **every** partition $P$,\n\n$$L(f,P) \\;\\le\\; \\underline{\\int_a^b} f \\;\\le\\; \\overline{\\int_a^b} f \\;\\le\\; U(f,P) ,$$\n\nthe outer inequalities because a member of a set is at most its supremum and at\nleast its infimum.\n\n### Integrability\n\n$f$ is **Darboux integrable on $[a,b]$**, and on this page simply\n**integrable**, when\n\n$$\\underline{\\int_a^b} f \\;=\\; \\overline{\\int_a^b} f ,$$\n\nand then the common value is written\n\n$$\\int_a^b f \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad \\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x ,$$\n\nthe **integral of $f$ over $[a,b]$**. It is a single well-determined real\nnumber, being the common value of two numbers each of which is unique\n([[lem-sup-unique]]). Without the displayed equality the symbol $\\int_a^b f$ is\nnot defined and is never written.\n\n**The inequality above is the whole difficulty.** By the previous paragraph\nintegrability is *never* a question of one integral exceeding the other, only of\nthe gap $\\overline{\\int_a^b} f - \\underline{\\int_a^b} f \\ge 0$ being $0$; and by\n[[thm-riemann-criterion]] that gap is $0$ exactly when a single partition can be\nfound making $U(f,P) - L(f,P)$ small. Whether that is possible is settled\ncompletely, in terms of the discontinuities of $f$, by\n[[thm-lebesgue-criterion]].\n\n**\"Riemann integrable\" means the same thing here.** The definition above is\nDarboux's. Riemann's own definition, in terms of tagged partitions of small\nmesh, is [[def-tagged-partition-and-riemann-sum]], and the two define the same\nclass of functions with the same integral by [[thm-darboux-equals-riemann]].\nUntil that theorem is proved the two phrases are kept apart; after it they are\nused interchangeably, as they are throughout the literature.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Applicable consumer restatements: F8: [F8] [[def-darboux-integral]] and [[thm-continuous-implies-integrable]]: continuous real functions on compact intervals have Riemann integrals, interpreted by the Darboux definition.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier status is published; this carrier has no proof section, as appropriate to its kind. No independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F8 is used in proof steps 1.1, 1.2, 3.1, 4.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-continuous-implies-integrable",
          "carrier": "items/thm-continuous-implies-integrable.md",
          "status": "Current carrier status: published; inspected for this dependency interface.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $a < b$ be reals and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be continuous on $[a,b]$\n(Continuity of $f : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ at a point of $A$ and on $A$: the $\\varepsilon$-$\\delta$ condition, its agreement with $\\lim_{x \\to c} f(x) = f(c)$ at a limit point, and continuity at an isolated point). Then $f$ is bounded (Lower bound, bounded below, bounded set) and Riemann\nintegrable on $[a,b]$ (The lower and upper Darboux integrals of a bounded $f$ on $[a,b]$ as $\\sup_P L(f,P)$ and $\\inf_P U(f,P)$, Darboux integrability as their equality, and the notation $\\int_a^b f$).\n\n**The proof gives more than integrability: it gives a partition that works.** For\nevery real $\\varepsilon > 0$ the uniform partition into $N$ parts already\nsatisfies $U(f,P) - L(f,P) < \\varepsilon$, as soon as $N$ is large enough that\n$(b-a)/\\iota(N)$ is below the $\\delta$ that uniform continuity supplies for\n$\\varepsilon/\\bigl(2(b-a)\\bigr)$. Uniform continuity is exactly what makes one\n$\\delta$ serve all $N$ subintervals at once, and it is the only place where the\ncompactness of $[a,b]$ is used.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Applicable consumer restatements: F8: [F8] [[def-darboux-integral]] and [[thm-continuous-implies-integrable]]: continuous real functions on compact intervals have Riemann integrals, interpreted by the Darboux definition.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier status is published; this carrier has no proof section, as appropriate to its kind. No independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F8 is used in proof steps 1.1, 1.2, 3.1, 4.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative",
          "carrier": "items/thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative.md",
          "status": "Current carrier status: published; inspected for this dependency interface.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $a<b$. Suppose $G:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b)$. If $f:[a,b]\\to\\mathbb R$ is Riemann integrable and\n\n$$f(x)=G'(x)\\qquad(a<x<b),$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\\int_a^b f=G(b)-G(a).$$\n\nNo derivative of $G$ at either endpoint is assumed, and the two endpoint values assigned to the integrable extension $f$ do not enter the conclusion.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Applicable consumer restatements: F9: [F9] [[thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative]]: if a real function is continuous on a closed interval, differentiable in its interior, and its derivative has a Riemann-integrable extension, the integral of that extension is the endpoint increment.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier status is published; this carrier has no proof section, as appropriate to its kind. No independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F9 is used in proof steps 1.1, 1.2, 3.1"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-derivative-of-a-power",
          "carrier": "items/lem-derivative-of-a-power.md",
          "status": "Current carrier status: published; inspected for this dependency interface.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Powers are those of Integer powers $a^m$, and $\\iota : \\mathbb{N} \\to\n\\mathbb{R}$ is the canonical natural of The canonical natural $\\iota(n) = n \\cdot 1_F$ of a field, so that\n$\\iota(0) = 0$ and $\\iota(m+1) = \\iota(m) + 1$. Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\n1. The function $p_0 : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$, $p_0(x) = x^{0}$, is the\n   constant function $1$, and it is differentiable at every $c \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n   with $p_0'(c) = 0$ (The derivative $f'(c) = \\lim_{x \\to c} \\frac{f(x) - f(c)}{x - c}$ of $f : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ at a point $c \\in A$ that is a limit point of $A$, and differentiability on a set).\n2. For $n \\ge 1$ the function $p_n : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$,\n   $p_n(x) = x^{n}$, is differentiable at every $c \\in \\mathbb{R}$, and\n   $$p_n'(c) \\;=\\; \\iota(n)\\,c^{\\,n-1} .$$\n3. For $n \\ge 1$ put $A_0 := \\{\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R} : x \\ne 0 \\,\\}$. The function\n   $m_n : A_0 \\to \\mathbb{R}$, $m_n(x) = x^{-n}$, is differentiable at every\n   $c \\in A_0$ as a function on $A_0$, and\n   $$m_n'(c) \\;=\\; -\\,\\iota(n)\\,c^{\\,-n-1} .$$\n4. Let $a : \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with $a_j = 0$ for $j \\ge n$, and let\n   $P : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be the polynomial function\n   $P(x) = \\sum_{j<n} a_j x^{j}$ (Finite sums and finite products, by recursion). Then $P$ is\n   differentiable at every $c \\in \\mathbb{R}$, and, defining $e : \\mathbb{N}\n   \\to \\mathbb{R}$ by $e_0 := 0$ and $e_j := \\iota(j)\\,a_j\\,c^{\\,j-1}$ for\n   $j \\ge 1$,\n   $$P'(c) \\;=\\; \\sum_{j<n} e_j .$$\n\n**Claim 2 is stated for $n \\ge 1$ and not for $n \\ge 0$, and that is not\ntimidity.** At $n = 0$ its right-hand side reads $\\iota(0)\\,c^{-1}$, and\n$c^{-1}$ is not defined at $c = 0$ (Integer powers $a^m$), so the formula is\nnot a statement about the whole line. Claim 1 is what covers $n = 0$, and it\nsays the derivative is $0$ there, which is what the informal reading\n\"$0 \\cdot c^{-1}$\" is reaching for. The same shift is why the term $e_0$ of\nclaim 4 is defined to be $0$ outright rather than by the formula.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "Applicable consumer restatements: F10: [F10] [[lem-derivative-of-a-power]]: for every integer $n\\geq0$, $s\\mapsto s^{n+1}$ is differentiable with derivative $(n+1)s^n$; constant scaling gives the corresponding monomial primitive.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier status is published; this carrier has no proof section, as appropriate to its kind. No independent approval is asserted.",
          "consumer_use": "F10 is used in proof steps 3.1"
        }
      ],
      "risk_review": {
        "status": "complete",
        "notes": "Checked each exact declared interface, its hypotheses and conventions, and its consuming use. This Step 1 record is not independent proof approval."
      },
      "repair_confidence": 1
    },
    "ex-nrqmx-finite-environment-recurrence": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "pthm-nrqmx-finite-reduction-channel",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In the finite product-state model, fix any $t\\geq0$ and define the complex-linear map $\\Phi_t:M_d(\\mathbb C)\\to M_d(\\mathbb C)$ by $\\Phi_t(X)=\\operatorname{Tr}_E\\!\\left(U_t(X\\otimes\\eta)U_t^*\\right)$. Then $\\Phi_t$ is a finite quantum channel. The family $t\\mapsto\\Phi_t$ need not obey the semigroup law and need not be CP-divisible. The terms “semigroup law” and “CP-divisible” have the definitions of [[def-nrqmx-finite-channel-families]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-nrqmx-finite-channel-families",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $d\\geq1$ and let $\\Phi_t:M_d(\\mathbb C)\\to M_d(\\mathbb C)$ be a family of finite quantum channels indexed by $t\\geq0$, where “finite quantum channel” has the meaning in [[def-qsm-dt-channel-definition]]. The family obeys the **semigroup law** when $\\Phi_0=\\operatorname{id}_{M_d(\\mathbb C)}$ and $\\Phi_{t+s}=\\Phi_t\\circ\\Phi_s$ for every $s,t\\geq0$.\n\nThe family is **CP-divisible** when for every $0\\leq s\\leq t$ there exists a finite quantum channel $V_{t,s}:M_d(\\mathbb C)\\to M_d(\\mathbb C)$ such that $\\Phi_t=V_{t,s}\\circ\\Phi_s$. CP-divisibility is this specified factorization property; the definition does not assert that it is a universal meaning of “Markovian”.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-sine-and-cosine-parametrize-the-unit-circle",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The map $t\\mapsto(\\cos t,\\sin t)$ is a bijection from $[0,2\\pi)$ onto $S^1=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:x^2+y^2=1\\}$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity]], [[thm-sine-cosine-signs-monotonicity-and-ranges]], [[thm-sine-cosine-zero-sets-and-fundamental-period]], [[def-pi-via-first-positive-cosine-zero]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For real $x,y$, $\\exp(x+iy)=e^x(\\cos y+i\\sin y)$ and $|\\exp(x+iy)|=e^x$. In particular $e^{i\\pi}+1=0$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[thm-complex-exponential-addition-and-real-extension]], [[thm-eulers-formula]], [[lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws]], [[thm-pythagorean-and-parity-identities-for-all-six-trigonometric-functions]], [[cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity]], [[thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas]], [[def-pi-via-first-positive-cosine-zero]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-complex-exponential-addition-and-real-extension",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For all $z,w\\in\\mathbb C$, $\\exp(z+w)=\\exp z\\exp w$. For real $x$, the complex value $\\exp(x+0i)$ equals the published real exponential $e^x$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[def-complex-exponential]], [[lem-complex-exponential-series-converges-everywhere]], [[lem-cauchy-product-of-absolutely-convergent-complex-series]], [[def-real-exponential-function-and-e]], [[lem-binomial-theorem-over-complex-numbers]], [[thm-binomial-closed-formula]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-complex-exponential",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\iota_{\\mathbb R}\\colon\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb R$ be the canonical-natural map\nof [[def-canonical-natural]], and let $j\\colon\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb C$ be the real\nembedding $j(x)=x+i0$ from [[def-complex-numbers-and-arithmetic]]. For every\n$n\\in\\mathbb N$, the factorial $n!$ is nonzero by\n[[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]], so\n$\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!)>0$ by [[lem-of-naturals-positive]] and consequently\n$j(\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!))\\ne0$ in the complex field\n[[thm-complex-numbers-form-a-field]].\n\nFor $z\\in\\mathbb C$, define\n$$ \\exp z:=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{z^n}{j(\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!))}. $$\nwhenever this complex series converges. Inside a complex expression we\nabbreviate the embedded denominator $j(\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!))$ by $n!$, so the\nsame definition may be written $\\exp z=\\sum z^n/n!$ without identifying a\nnatural number with a complex number. Powers and series are those of\n[[def-complex-integer-powers]] and\n[[def-complex-series-power-series-and-absolute-convergence]]. The convergence\nfor every $z\\in\\mathbb C$ is discharged by\n[[lem-complex-exponential-series-converges-everywhere]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "lem-qsm-matrix-state-calculus",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Use the following conventions on a complex inner-product space $H$ of dimension $D\\geq1$, with inner product linear in its first argument. For an endomorphism $A$, define $\\operatorname{Tr}A:=\\sum_{j=1}^D\\langle Ae_j,e_j\\rangle$ in an orthonormal basis; step 3.1 proves basis independence. For any Hermitian $M$, its **positive spectral subspace** (called its positive support here) is $S_M:=\\bigoplus_{\\lambda>0}\\ker(M-\\lambda I)$; when $M\\geq0$ this equals $(\\ker M)^\\perp$. Its support logarithm is $\\log(M|_{S_M}):=\\sum_{\\lambda>0}(\\log\\lambda)P_\\lambda|_{S_M}$, where $P_\\lambda$ is the orthogonal projection onto that eigenspace. No value $\\log0$ is assigned; for $M=0$ this is the unique endomorphism of the zero space. For the set $\\mathcal D(H)$ of positive semidefinite Hermitian endomorphisms with trace one, a point $\\rho$ is **extreme** if every equality $\\rho=t\\sigma+(1-t)\\tau$ with $\\sigma,\\tau\\in\\mathcal D(H)$ and $0<t<1$ implies $\\sigma=\\tau=\\rho$.\n\nLet $H$ be a complex inner-product space of finite dimension $D\\geq1$, with inner product linear in its first argument. Every Hermitian endomorphism $M$ has an orthonormal eigenbasis with real eigenvalues. For a real function $f$ on its finite spectrum, define $f(M)$ by its spectral projections; this definition is independent of the choice of eigenbasis. In particular positive square roots, exponentials, and logarithms on positive supports are well defined.\n\nFor a density matrix $\\rho$ on $H$, meaning a Hermitian positive semidefinite operator with $\\operatorname{Tr}\\rho=1$, $\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho A)=\\sum_jp_j\\langle Au_j,u_j\\rangle$ for bounded $A$, where $\\rho=\\sum_jp_j|u_j\\rangle\\langle u_j|$. The weighted form $(X,Y)_\\rho=\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho X^*Y)$ satisfies Cauchy–Schwarz, $|(X,Y)_\\rho|^2\\leq(X,X)_\\rho(Y,Y)_\\rho$. Finally, $\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho^2)\\leq1$, with equality exactly for rank-one densities, and those are exactly the extreme points of the finite density-matrix set.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "In the environment $Z$ eigenbasis $|b\\rangle$, $b=\\pm1$, the finite spectral calculus [F7] gives $U_t=\\sum_{b=\\pm1}V_b(t)\\otimes|b\\rangle\\langle b|$ with $V_b(t)=\\operatorname{diag}(e^{-ibgt},e^{ibgt})$. By [F4], each diagonal phase has modulus one, so these blocks and $U_t$ are unitary. The environment state has equal weight on $b=\\pm1$, so the reduced map is $\\Phi_t(X)=\\frac12(V_{+1}XV_{+1}^*+V_{-1}XV_{-1}^*)$; multiplying the two diagonal matrices shows that the off-diagonal entries are multiplied by $\\kappa(t)=\\frac12(e^{-2igt}+e^{2igt})=\\cos(2gt)$, while the diagonal entries are unchanged.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "By [F3] choose $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$ with $\\cos\\theta=0$ and $\\sin\\theta=1$, and put $s=\\theta/(2g)>0$. Then [F4] gives $e^{i\\theta}=i$, while [F5] and [F6] with $e^{i\\theta}e^{-i\\theta}=e^0=1$ give $e^{-i\\theta}=-i$; another application of [F5] yields $\\kappa(s)=0$ and $\\kappa(2s)=\\frac12(e^{-2i\\theta}+e^{2i\\theta})=-1$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Let $X_P=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\rho_\\pm=(I\\pm X_P)/2$. Since $X_P=X_P^*$, $X_P^2=I$, and $\\operatorname{Tr}X_P=0$, each $\\rho_\\pm$ is a positive trace-one matrix. Steps 1.1 and 1.2 give $\\Phi_s(\\rho_+)=\\Phi_s(\\rho_-)=I/2$, but $\\Phi_{2s}(\\rho_+)=\\rho_-$ and $\\Phi_{2s}(\\rho_-)=\\rho_+$. Thus $\\Phi_{2s}(\\rho_+)\\ne\\Phi_s(\\Phi_s(\\rho_+))$, so the semigroup law in [F2] fails. If a channel $V_{2s,s}$ satisfied $\\Phi_{2s}=V_{2s,s}\\circ\\Phi_s$, it would map the equal intermediate states to equal outputs, contradicting their distinct final states; by [F1], every $\\Phi_t$ is a channel, so the family is not CP-divisible by [F2].",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
