    "ex-nrqmx-amplitude-damping-jump-law": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "pthm-nrqmx-amplitude-damping-model",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For the separately selected qubit GKSL model, let $H=\\hbar\\omega|1\\rangle\\langle1|$ with $\\omega\\in\\mathbb R$ in $\\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ and $\\hbar>0$ in joule-seconds, and let $L=\\sqrt{\\gamma}|0\\rangle\\langle1|$ with $\\gamma\\geq0$ in $\\mathrm{s}^{-1}$, so $L$ has units $\\mathrm{s}^{-1/2}$. For any initial density matrix $\\rho(0)$ in the ordered basis $(|0\\rangle,|1\\rangle)$, the solution $\\rho(t)=e^{t\\mathcal L}\\rho(0)$ for $t\\geq0$ satisfies $\\rho_{11}(t)=e^{-\\gamma t}\\rho_{11}(0)$, $\\rho_{00}(t)=\\rho_{00}(0)+(1-e^{-\\gamma t})\\rho_{11}(0)$, $\\rho_{01}(t)=e^{(i\\omega-\\gamma/2)t}\\rho_{01}(0)$, and $\\rho_{10}(t)=e^{(-i\\omega-\\gamma/2)t}\\rho_{10}(0)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-nrqmx-finite-jump-trajectories",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $d\\geq1$, let $r\\geq0$ be an integer, let $H=H^*\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$ have units joules, let $\\hbar>0$ have units joule-seconds, and let $L_1,\\ldots,L_r\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$ have units $\\mathrm{s}^{-1/2}$, with the finite GKSL convention of [[def-qsm-dt-gksl-definition]]. Put $K=-iH/\\hbar-\\frac12\\sum_{a=1}^rL_a^*L_a$ and $R(u)=e^{uK}$ for $u\\geq0$, with the exponential defined by its norm-convergent power series. For a density matrix $\\rho\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$, meaning $\\rho=\\rho^*\\geq0$ and $\\operatorname{Tr}\\rho=1$, define the no-jump branch at time $t\\geq0$ by $W_{\\varnothing,\\varnothing}(t)=R(t)$ and its branch state by $W_{\\varnothing,\\varnothing}(t)\\rho W_{\\varnothing,\\varnothing}(t)^*$.\n\nAll time variables $u,t,s_1,\\ldots,s_n$ are measured in seconds. For $n\\geq1$, a jump-history label is $\\mathbf a=(a_1,\\ldots,a_n)\\in\\{1,\\ldots,r\\}^n$ and its jump times are seconds $0<s_1<\\cdots<s_n<t$. Define the corresponding branch operator by $W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)=R(t-s_n)L_{a_n}R(s_n-s_{n-1})\\cdots L_{a_2}R(s_2-s_1)L_{a_1}R(s_1)$ and the unnormalized branch state by $W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)\\rho W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)^*$. Its branch-weight density is $w_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)=\\operatorname{Tr}(W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)\\rho W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)^*)$. The ordered-simplex integral of this density means the repeated Riemann integral $\\int_0^t ds_n\\int_0^{s_n}ds_{n-1}\\cdots\\int_0^{s_2}ds_1$, applied componentwise to matrices; each continuous real component is integrable on the compact integration interval. For $n\\geq1$, $W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}$ has units $\\mathrm{s}^{-n/2}$ and $w_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}$ has units $\\mathrm{s}^{-n}$; integrating over the $n$ time variables gives a dimensionless weight. If $r=0$, there are no branches with $n\\geq1$. This definition alone does not assert that the branch weights sum to one.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "pthm-nrqmx-jump-unravelling-finite",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $d\\geq1$, let $H=H^*\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$ have units joules, let $\\hbar>0$ have units joule-seconds, and let $L_1,\\ldots,L_r\\in M_d(\\mathbb C)$ be a finite family with units $\\mathrm{s}^{-1/2}$, where $r\\geq0$. For the selected time-homogeneous finite GKSL model, put $\\mathcal L(X)=-\\frac{i}{\\hbar}[H,X]+\\sum_{a=1}^r(L_aXL_a^*-\\frac12\\{L_a^*L_a,X\\})$. With $K=-iH/\\hbar-\\frac12\\sum_aL_a^*L_a$ and the finite branch operators of [[def-nrqmx-finite-jump-trajectories]], each no-jump operator $R(u)=e^{uK}$ is a contraction, each branch state $W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)\\rho W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)^*$ is positive for $\\rho\\geq0$, and for every density matrix $\\rho$ and $t\\geq0$,\n\n$$e^{t\\mathcal L}(\\rho)=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\ \\sum_{\\mathbf a\\in\\{1,\\ldots,r\\}^n}\\ \\int_{0<s_1<\\cdots<s_n<t}W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)\\rho W_{\\mathbf a,\\mathbf s}(t)^*\\,ds_1\\cdots ds_n,$$\n\nwhere the $n=0$ term is $R(t)\\rho R(t)^*$ and branches with $n\\geq1$ are absent when $r=0$. The series converges in trace norm, uniformly on each bounded time interval; the sum of all branch weights is one. This is an ensemble representation of the finite GKSL solution, not a unique microscopic trajectory or a claim about a particular detector.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-complex-exponential-addition-and-real-extension",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For all $z,w\\in\\mathbb C$, $\\exp(z+w)=\\exp z\\exp w$. For real $x$, the complex value $\\exp(x+0i)$ equals the published real exponential $e^x$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[def-complex-exponential]], [[lem-complex-exponential-series-converges-everywhere]], [[lem-cauchy-product-of-absolutely-convergent-complex-series]], [[def-real-exponential-function-and-e]], [[lem-binomial-theorem-over-complex-numbers]], [[thm-binomial-closed-formula]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-exponential-limits-and-range",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "$$\\exp(x)\\to+\\infty\\quad(x\\to+\\infty),\\qquad \\exp(x)\\to0\\quad(x\\to-\\infty),$$\nand the range of $\\exp$ is contained in $(0,\\infty)$ and is unbounded above with infimum $0$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "For $L=\\sqrt{\\gamma}|0\\rangle\\langle1|$, the no-jump generator is $K=\\operatorname{diag}(0,-i\\omega-\\gamma/2)$. Hence $R(t)|1\\rangle=e^{-(i\\omega+\\gamma/2)t}|1\\rangle$ and $R(t)|0\\rangle=|0\\rangle$. For the initial state $|1\\rangle\\langle1|$, the no-jump branch weight is $\\operatorname{Tr}(R(t)|1\\rangle\\langle1|R(t)^*)=e^{-\\gamma t}$, equal to the excited population in [F1].",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "For $\\gamma>0$ and a one-jump history at $s\\in(0,T)$, $R(T-s)LR(s)|1\\rangle=\\sqrt{\\gamma}\\,e^{-(i\\omega+\\gamma/2)s}|0\\rangle$, since $L|1\\rangle=\\sqrt{\\gamma}|0\\rangle$ and $R(T-s)|0\\rangle=|0\\rangle$. Its branch-weight density from [F2] is $\\gamma e^{-\\gamma s}$. Because $L|0\\rangle=0$ and $R(u)|0\\rangle=|0\\rangle$, every branch with at least two jumps has zero weight. The normalized ensemble [F3] therefore gives $\\int_0^T\\gamma e^{-\\gamma s}\\,ds=1-e^{-\\gamma T}$: the only nonzero contributions are the no-jump weight $e^{-\\gamma T}$ from step 1.1 and the one-jump integral. Letting $T\\to\\infty$, [F4] identifies this as the real exponential and [F5] gives $e^{-\\gamma T}\\to0$ because $\\gamma>0$; hence the first-jump density has total mass one. Each first jump leaves the normalized state $|0\\rangle\\langle0|$. If $\\gamma=0$, then $L=0$, all positive-jump branches vanish, and the no-jump weight is one.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
